00:18:53,133 --> 00:18:54,333
- Hey, the other way,
the other way,

499
00:18:54,400 --> 00:18:57,003
the other way, Shep!

500
00:18:57,070 --> 00:19:00,773
No, no, go dig, dig.
- No, no, Shep.

501
00:19:00,840 --> 00:19:02,775
- Dig, keep digging.

502
00:19:02,843 --> 00:19:04,477
That’s million-dollar dirt.

503
00:19:04,545 --> 00:19:06,180
We’ve hit it this time, Shep.

504
00:19:06,247 --> 00:19:10,017
We’ve hit it sure.
No, keep going.

505
00:19:10,083 --> 00:19:12,085
I forgot to peer the shaft up.

506
00:19:12,152 --> 00:19:13,987
I’ve darn near been
the richest corpse

507
00:19:14,053 --> 00:19:15,322
in the territory, ha, ha.

508
00:19:15,388 --> 00:19:17,090
- He had sand on his lap,
and he needed help?

509
00:19:17,157 --> 00:19:18,992
( CHUCKLING )
- Come on, girl.

510
00:19:19,058 --> 00:19:20,893
We’re heading for town.

511
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:23,263
Just wait ’til you see
Frank Blake’s eyes

512
00:19:23,330 --> 00:19:25,298
when we tell him
he’s a millionaire.

513
00:19:25,365 --> 00:19:26,800
- You’re welcome.

514
00:19:26,867 --> 00:19:28,635
( Music playing )

515
00:19:28,702 --> 00:19:30,170
- ♪ Nothing to do

516
00:19:30,237 --> 00:19:31,505
- ♪ Nothing to do ♪
- ♪ There’s nothing to do ♪

517
00:19:31,572 --> 00:19:33,440
- ♪ Nothing to do
- Nothing to do ♪

518
00:19:33,507 --> 00:19:36,310
- ♪ Nothing to do
- Nothing to do ♪

519
00:19:36,377 --> 00:19:37,677
- Oh, careful,
there’s a speed trap here.

520
00:19:37,743 --> 00:19:40,447
( Shep barking )
( Children shouting )

521
00:19:40,513 --> 00:19:42,815
- Thanks for the treaty, mister!

522
00:19:42,883 --> 00:19:44,483
- Thanks for the continent!

523
00:19:44,552 --> 00:19:46,253
- This was a time
when Denver Pyles

524
00:19:46,320 --> 00:19:47,955
roamed the land freely.

525
00:19:50,223 --> 00:19:51,492
- ♪ Ding ding ding
Ding ding ding ♪

526
00:19:51,558 --> 00:19:53,327
- Eggnog--
make it a double.

527
00:19:53,393 --> 00:19:54,828
- Well, Jonathan,
how are you?

528
00:19:54,895 --> 00:19:55,828
Merry Christmas.

529
00:19:55,895 --> 00:19:56,897
- And get your dog
out of here.

530
00:19:56,963 --> 00:19:58,032
- How about it,
on the house?

531
00:19:58,098 --> 00:19:59,065
- No.

532
00:19:59,132 --> 00:20:00,500
- Well, how about you, pup?

533
00:20:00,567 --> 00:20:02,235
- Jim Beam, rocks.

534
00:20:03,737 --> 00:20:05,672
- Can’t call you pup anymore,
can I, fella?

535
00:20:05,738 --> 00:20:07,173
- Can’t call her a fella,
neither.

536
00:20:07,240 --> 00:20:09,108
- She’s neutered.
- Say, that’s right.

537
00:20:09,175 --> 00:20:10,810
Excuse me, beautiful.

538
00:20:10,877 --> 00:20:12,012
- She’s looking
right at his throat.

539
00:20:12,078 --> 00:20:13,213
- Well, how’s it
going, Jonathan?

540
00:20:13,280 --> 00:20:14,313
- Shep and me
ain’t complaining.

541
00:20:14,380 --> 00:20:15,582
- Well, I am.

542
00:20:15,648 --> 00:20:17,750
- Uh, Steve,
has Frank been around tonight?

543
00:20:17,817 --> 00:20:19,352
- Frank?

544
00:20:19,418 --> 00:20:21,888
Frank Blake?

545
00:20:21,955 --> 00:20:23,123
Be right back.

546
00:20:23,190 --> 00:20:24,825
- I’ll go see
if Miss Kitty’s here.

547
00:20:24,892 --> 00:20:26,660
- Thanks, Steve.

548
00:20:26,727 --> 00:20:27,727
Deal me out.

549
00:20:27,793 --> 00:20:29,028
- Hi, folks.

550
00:20:31,798 --> 00:20:33,167
- Jonathan Harvey?

551
00:20:33,233 --> 00:20:35,335
- Hm?
- What, what is-- oh.

552
00:20:35,402 --> 00:20:37,003
- That’s right.
I’m Lin Taylor.

553
00:20:37,070 --> 00:20:38,438
- I love your work.

554
00:20:38,505 --> 00:20:40,040
- Sorry we couldn’t
let you know, Jonathan.

555
00:20:40,107 --> 00:20:41,475
Martha wanted to,

556
00:20:41,542 --> 00:20:43,510
but there was no way
of finding you in time.

557
00:20:43,577 --> 00:20:45,045
- Time for what?

558
00:20:45,112 --> 00:20:48,015
- Frank’s dead, Jonathan,
over a month ago of pneumonia.

559
00:20:48,082 --> 00:20:51,017
- Frank, hm.
Frank, hm, hm, hm.

560
00:20:53,520 --> 00:20:54,453
Frank, hm.

561
00:20:54,520 --> 00:20:55,988
- Tommy worries me, Jonathan.

562
00:20:56,055 --> 00:20:57,357
- Tommy?
Who’s Tommy?

563
00:20:57,423 --> 00:20:59,158
- Do you know
that he’s never once cried?

564
00:20:59,225 --> 00:21:00,660
- Ah, just hit him
with a class ring.

565
00:21:00,727 --> 00:21:02,997
- If he could just let it out.

566
00:21:03,063 --> 00:21:06,333
- We’ll work it out, Martha.
We’ll find a way.

567
00:21:06,400 --> 00:21:10,403
But right now,
what about this fella, Taylor?

568
00:21:10,470 --> 00:21:13,140
- Lin’s a fine man.
He’s been wonderful.

569
00:21:13,207 --> 00:21:15,242
- Why did Frank take him in
as a partner?

570
00:21:15,308 --> 00:21:17,043
- He had to.

571
00:21:17,110 --> 00:21:20,913
Lin’s buying in when he did
kept us from losing everything.

572
00:21:20,980 --> 00:21:22,948
- I see.
- Now, who’s Lin?

573
00:21:23,017 --> 00:21:25,218
- Does that make him a partner
of grub stake rights?

574
00:21:25,285 --> 00:21:26,953
- Yes, why?

575
00:21:28,288 --> 00:21:30,557
Jonathan,
you’ve made a strike!

576
00:21:30,623 --> 00:21:31,958
- I’ve got something
to show you.

577
00:21:32,025 --> 00:21:32,958
- No!
- No!

578
00:21:33,027 --> 00:21:34,227
- Ooh!

579
00:21:34,293 --> 00:21:36,028
- Oh, it’s okay.
( KNOCK ON DOOR )

580
00:21:36,095 --> 00:21:38,065
It’s the feds!

581
00:21:38,132 --> 00:21:40,100
- Now, is this the real old west
or the Roy Rogers old west--

582
00:21:40,167 --> 00:21:41,368
- Come in.

583
00:21:41,435 --> 00:21:42,535
- --where they had
electricity and cars.

584
00:21:42,602 --> 00:21:44,337
- Lin, Jonathan’s inside.

585
00:21:44,403 --> 00:21:45,538
I’m so glad you’re here.

586
00:21:45,605 --> 00:21:47,073
Jonathan has something
to show us.

587
00:21:47,140 --> 00:21:49,843
- He’s the world’s largest elf.

588
00:21:49,910 --> 00:21:50,877
- Yeah.

589
00:21:52,445 --> 00:21:53,380
Look at this.

590
00:21:53,447 --> 00:21:54,447
- A piece of key lime pie, hm.

591
00:21:54,513 --> 00:21:55,648
- I found a vein of that.

592
00:21:55,715 --> 00:21:57,183
If it’s deep enough,

593
00:21:57,250 --> 00:21:59,285
it could be the biggest strike
this territory has ever seen.

594
00:21:59,352 --> 00:22:01,588
That’s why we’ve got to
file a claim and quick.

595
00:22:01,655 --> 00:22:03,623
- File before it’s proved?

596
00:22:03,690 --> 00:22:05,258
You file now and word gets out
that you struck ore like this--

597
00:22:05,325 --> 00:22:06,660
- Who’s that guy?

598
00:22:06,727 --> 00:22:08,028
- --every claim jumper
in the territory

599
00:22:08,095 --> 00:22:09,797
will be in those hills
in a week,

600
00:22:09,863 --> 00:22:12,665
before you’re sure yourself
if you’ve hit the main vein.

601
00:22:12,732 --> 00:22:14,702
- By gad, you’re right.

602
00:22:16,068 --> 00:22:17,670
Thirty years
of mud scrabbling;

603
00:22:17,737 --> 00:22:19,773
and when I do find
the glory dust,

604
00:22:19,840 --> 00:22:20,973
I go off half-cocked.

605
00:22:21,040 --> 00:22:22,242
- But enough vernacular.

606
00:22:22,308 --> 00:22:23,510
- Mr. Taylor--
- I love you.

607
00:22:23,577 --> 00:22:24,778
- Martha’s right about you.

608
00:22:24,845 --> 00:22:25,945
- You’re odd.

609
00:22:26,012 --> 00:22:27,380
- And right now
you can start working

610
00:22:27,447 --> 00:22:30,217
and being a partner
by lining me up some supplies.

611
00:22:30,283 --> 00:22:32,118
- Of course, Jonathan,
anything you need.

612
00:22:32,185 --> 00:22:34,287
- Meet you in the store
in a half hour.

613
00:22:34,353 --> 00:22:35,755
- Yeah,
I live to serve you, Cal.

614
00:22:35,822 --> 00:22:37,923
- Goodnight, Lin.
- What did he stop by for?

615
00:22:37,990 --> 00:22:39,458
- If it’s all right with you,
Martha--

616
00:22:39,525 --> 00:22:40,760
- I’d like to know
who that guy was.

617
00:22:40,827 --> 00:22:43,028
- I’d like to talk right
with Tommy.

618
00:22:43,097 --> 00:22:45,932
- All right, Jonathan.
If there’s anything you can do.

619
00:22:45,998 --> 00:22:48,602
- Shame him, humiliate him,
whatever you think is best.

620
00:22:50,070 --> 00:22:52,405
- I’m gonna snap this kid’s will
like an old pretzel.

621
00:22:52,472 --> 00:22:55,275
( Sad music playing )

622
00:22:59,278 --> 00:23:02,515
- Come on, now, cry, cry!

623
00:23:02,582 --> 00:23:04,417
- Tommy.
- Can you hear me?

624
00:23:04,483 --> 00:23:06,352
Can you feel me near you?

625
00:23:06,420 --> 00:23:08,922
- What’s Rutherford B. Hayes
doing in this movie?

626
00:23:08,988 --> 00:23:10,290
- Tommy.

627
00:23:10,357 --> 00:23:12,092
- Hm?

628
00:23:12,158 --> 00:23:13,327
Jonathan,

629
00:23:14,460 --> 00:23:15,528
Jonathan.

630
00:23:15,595 --> 00:23:18,265
- Jonathan.
- Oh, Jonathan!

631
00:23:18,332 --> 00:23:19,598
Why’d he have to die?

632
00:23:19,665 --> 00:23:21,000
- Ah, you must’ve done
something wrong, Tommy.

633
00:23:21,067 --> 00:23:22,835
- I can’t say.

634
00:23:22,902 --> 00:23:25,838
- I want my pa, Jonathan!
I want pa!

635
00:23:25,905 --> 00:23:27,173
- You know,
his head just doesn’t have

636
00:23:27,240 --> 00:23:28,642
the structural support
for that hair.

637
00:23:28,708 --> 00:23:30,877
- Some things
we can’t answer, Tommy.

638
00:23:30,943 --> 00:23:32,345
- God check time, Tommy!

639
00:23:32,412 --> 00:23:34,948
- The Lord’s got His way
of doing things

640
00:23:35,015 --> 00:23:36,883
that we just can’t answer.

641
00:23:38,352 --> 00:23:40,487
- But Jonathan,
isn’t pre-determination based on

642
00:23:40,553 --> 00:23:42,155
not an egocentric conception
of a--

643
00:23:42,222 --> 00:23:44,023
- I didn’t want to cry.

644
00:23:44,090 --> 00:23:45,225
- You go ahead and cry.

645
00:23:45,292 --> 00:23:46,493
- You baby.

646
00:23:46,560 --> 00:23:48,528
- Used to do it myself
sometimes,

647
00:23:48,595 --> 00:23:50,530
before my tears
all dried up on me.

648
00:23:50,597 --> 00:23:51,965
- Not like this.

649
00:23:52,032 --> 00:23:54,267
- Now how about Shep here?

650
00:23:54,333 --> 00:23:57,603
Those hills up there,
that’s no place for a dog.

651
00:23:57,670 --> 00:24:00,007
- Hi, star of the film here.
- Least wise, not Shep.

652
00:24:00,073 --> 00:24:01,575
- It isn’t?

653
00:24:01,642 --> 00:24:06,012
- No, no, it’s too lonesome.
She likes folks around.

654
00:24:06,078 --> 00:24:09,482
The fact is she’s just plain
ain’t happy up there.

655
00:24:09,548 --> 00:24:11,985
- You guys talking about me?

656
00:24:12,052 --> 00:24:15,122
- As for me, well,
I’m better off alone.

657
00:24:15,188 --> 00:24:16,388
- That’s for sure.

658
00:24:16,455 --> 00:24:17,757
- Lots of times,

659
00:24:17,823 --> 00:24:20,093
the dog is nothing
but a darned bother to me.

660
00:24:20,160 --> 00:24:21,260
- Oh, must you air
our dirty laundry?

661
00:24:21,327 --> 00:24:23,230
- Bothers you?

662
00:24:23,297 --> 00:24:24,697
Shep?

663
00:24:24,763 --> 00:24:27,233
- Yeah-- you, Shep,
now go on, go on now.

664
00:24:27,300 --> 00:24:30,003
Here, you get away.

665
00:24:30,070 --> 00:24:31,505
Now go on, get away.

666
00:24:31,572 --> 00:24:33,105
- Sorry, Tommy.

667
00:24:33,173 --> 00:24:36,142
- Now, what I’m sand-washing
up to with all this is--

668
00:24:36,208 --> 00:24:37,377
- Can you take over
her payments?

669
00:24:37,443 --> 00:24:39,078
- How about taking her
off my hands?

670
00:24:39,145 --> 00:24:40,913
- Oh, that’s a great replacement
for my father.

671
00:24:40,980 --> 00:24:42,248
- Will you do it, Tommy,
will you?

672
00:24:42,315 --> 00:24:44,417
- You’re an old liar, Jonathan.
- Hm?

673
00:24:44,483 --> 00:24:46,418
- I can’t take Shep from you.

674
00:24:46,485 --> 00:24:50,323
She’s your dog, nobody else’s,
’til she dies.

675
00:24:50,390 --> 00:24:51,992
Thanks anyway.

676
00:24:52,058 --> 00:24:54,593
And look,
don’t tell anybody I bawled.

677
00:24:54,660 --> 00:24:56,997
- Sure, you little wuss.
- Will you, please?

678
00:24:57,063 --> 00:24:58,798
- Honest to Christmas.

679
00:25:00,033 --> 00:25:01,500
- Hey, everybody,
Tommy’s in there

680
00:25:01,568 --> 00:25:03,737
crying like a little girl!

681
00:25:03,803 --> 00:25:05,038
- We didn’t do so good.

682
00:25:06,272 --> 00:25:07,973
- Look, I’ve really
gotta go outside.

683
00:25:08,040 --> 00:25:10,043
- All right, it’s Christmas,

684
00:25:10,110 --> 00:25:12,212
and giving
is part of Christmas.

685
00:25:12,278 --> 00:25:13,680
- Snausages.

686
00:25:13,747 --> 00:25:15,615
JONATHAN: Anyway, you are
a bother to me.

687
00:25:17,250 --> 00:25:18,183
( Shep whining )

688
00:25:18,252 --> 00:25:19,852
Ah, be quiet.

689
00:25:19,920 --> 00:25:21,453
Don’t look at me that way!

690
00:25:32,532 --> 00:25:34,233
- Tonight on "The Advocates,"

691
00:25:34,300 --> 00:25:37,870
our debate will be about
the woman from today’s short.

692
00:25:37,937 --> 00:25:41,073
Tom Servo and Crow T. Robot
will argue over

693
00:25:41,140 --> 00:25:43,277
whether she’s more attractive
sloppy--

694
00:25:43,343 --> 00:25:44,310
- Yes.
- --or clean.

695
00:25:44,377 --> 00:25:45,612
- Clean.

696
00:25:45,678 --> 00:25:47,147
- Representing sloppy
will be Crow.

697
00:25:47,213 --> 00:25:48,213
- Yes.

698
00:25:48,282 --> 00:25:50,317
- Representing clean
will be Servo.

699
00:25:50,383 --> 00:25:51,317
- Thank you.
- Yeah.

700
00:25:51,383 --> 00:25:53,420
- I will be your moderator.

701
00:25:53,487 --> 00:25:56,388
The outcome will be decided
by Joel Robinson.

702
00:25:56,455 --> 00:25:58,458
I now turn the floor
over to Crow.

703
00:25:58,525 --> 00:26:00,160
( CLEARING THROAT )
- Uh, thank you, Gypsy.

704
00:26:00,227 --> 00:26:03,330
( CLEARING THROAT )
I like her sloppy.

705
00:26:03,397 --> 00:26:06,065
While her well-groomed,
in other words square,

706
00:26:06,132 --> 00:26:08,568
classmates were listening
to Pat Boone and Patti Page,

707
00:26:08,635 --> 00:26:10,403
she was at the local jazz club

708
00:26:10,470 --> 00:26:12,805
grooving to Miles, Monk,
and Coltrane.

709
00:26:12,872 --> 00:26:15,008
While her classmates
were struggling to make it

710
00:26:15,075 --> 00:26:16,610
through an issue
of Reader’s Digest,

711
00:26:16,677 --> 00:26:18,578
she was the only woman
on campus

712
00:26:18,645 --> 00:26:20,580
who could freely quote
Henry Miller.

713
00:26:20,647 --> 00:26:21,815
She may be sloppy,

714
00:26:21,882 --> 00:26:25,185
but she fits neatly
into my idea of paradise.

715
00:26:25,252 --> 00:26:26,385
Thank you.

716
00:26:26,452 --> 00:26:28,688
- Thank you.
Tom Servo.

717
00:26:28,755 --> 00:26:30,423
- ( CLEARING THROAT )
Thank you, Gypsy.

718
00:26:30,490 --> 00:26:32,392
I like her clean,
’cause it just shows

719
00:26:32,458 --> 00:26:35,328
that she wants to change
the system from within.

720
00:26:35,395 --> 00:26:37,630
Sure, she’s a seething
cauldron of passion,

721
00:26:37,697 --> 00:26:39,332
but she wears
clean underpants,

722
00:26:39,398 --> 00:26:41,567
and she knows where her shirt is
in the morning.

723
00:26:41,635 --> 00:26:44,370
Call her what you will:
a Scoop Jackson Democrat

724
00:26:44,437 --> 00:26:46,138
or a Jacob Javits Republican.

725
00:26:46,205 --> 00:26:49,208
Either way, she’s the stuff
that dreams are made of.

726
00:26:49,275 --> 00:26:50,210
Thank you.

727
00:26:50,277 --> 00:26:51,510
- Thank you, boys.
- Thank you.

728
00:26:51,577 --> 00:26:53,613
- And now Joel Robinson
will render his verdict.

729
00:26:53,680 --> 00:26:55,482
Joel.
- Well, thank you, Gypsy.

730
00:26:55,548 --> 00:26:58,117
Uh, both of these issues
are complex,

731
00:26:58,183 --> 00:27:01,153
and there are no easy answers,
but Crow is right.

732
00:27:01,220 --> 00:27:03,022
- Woo-hoo!
I’m right, I’m right! Hoo-hoo!

733
00:27:03,088 --> 00:27:05,392
- Look, ho, ho, ho, ho, ho,
whoa, whoa!

734
00:27:05,458 --> 00:27:06,893
Wait a minute, Joel!

735
00:27:06,960 --> 00:27:08,928
Now, the whole subject of
cleanliness and grooming

736
00:27:08,995 --> 00:27:10,363
is very important to me,

737
00:27:10,430 --> 00:27:12,132
and I know a much more
effective way

738
00:27:12,198 --> 00:27:13,467
to resolve this issue.

739
00:27:13,533 --> 00:27:14,800
- Well, uh, how’s that?

740
00:27:14,867 --> 00:27:15,802
- A spitting contest.

741
00:27:15,868 --> 00:27:17,437
( SHOUTING )
- Ow, ooh, oh, hey!

742
00:27:17,503 --> 00:27:18,872
- Wait, wait.

743
00:27:18,938 --> 00:27:20,507
Hey, knock it off. Hey!

744
00:27:20,573 --> 00:27:24,043
( Theme music playing )

745
00:27:28,948 --> 00:27:30,850
- Don’t even say it.

746
00:27:30,917 --> 00:27:33,152
- But I wanted a super soaker.

747
00:27:33,218 --> 00:27:35,355
- Aw, gee, that Jonathan.

748
00:27:35,422 --> 00:27:36,923
- Well, that son of a--
ha, ha!

749
00:27:36,990 --> 00:27:37,990
- Merry Christmas, Tommy.

750
00:27:38,057 --> 00:27:39,525
- Merry Christmas, Ma.

751
00:27:39,592 --> 00:27:40,527
Look!

752
00:27:40,593 --> 00:27:42,128
- Yes, dear, I know.

753
00:27:42,195 --> 00:27:44,630
- He’s got mange, distemper,
and lots of ticks.

754
00:27:44,697 --> 00:27:47,133
- "Dear Tommy,
I put it up to Shep.

755
00:27:47,200 --> 00:27:49,835
She agreed with me.
She’s yours.

756
00:27:49,902 --> 00:27:52,538
Merry Christmas
from Jonathan."

757
00:27:52,605 --> 00:27:53,740
Where is Jonathan?

758
00:27:53,807 --> 00:27:55,575
- He’s dead.
Open your other gifts.

759
00:27:55,642 --> 00:27:57,443
- He’s gone.
- Gone?

760
00:27:59,045 --> 00:28:00,647
Is it all right, Ma?

761
00:28:00,713 --> 00:28:02,215
Can I really keep her?

762
00:28:02,282 --> 00:28:03,783
- That’s what he wanted, Tommy.

763
00:28:03,850 --> 00:28:06,385
- Thanks, lady,
you were my last hope.

764
00:28:09,655 --> 00:28:13,025
- Oh, man, shouldn’t have eaten
all those socks.

765
00:28:13,092 --> 00:28:15,695
- Divorced, harassed
by creditors.

766
00:28:17,697 --> 00:28:21,100
- Come on, pretend
that you like me, girl.

767
00:28:21,167 --> 00:28:24,270
- Oh, man, I shouldn’t have
eaten all those socks, ugh.

768
00:28:24,337 --> 00:28:26,505
( Thundering )

769
00:28:26,572 --> 00:28:28,708
- Heathcliff, Heathcliff!

770
00:28:28,775 --> 00:28:29,742
Arf!

771
00:28:31,443 --> 00:28:34,413
( Humming tune from
"the great escape" )

772
00:28:37,350 --> 00:28:39,752
- No one escapes
from Stalag 13.

773
00:28:41,520 --> 00:28:43,590
TOMMY:
Shep, oh, Shep.

774
00:28:43,657 --> 00:28:44,790
- Come on,
it’s dress-up time.

775
00:28:44,857 --> 00:28:46,625
Tonight, you’re
Marlene Dietrich.

776
00:28:49,028 --> 00:28:50,963
- Shep.
- You’re drunk, aren’t you?

777
00:28:51,030 --> 00:28:52,565
Bad dog!

778
00:28:52,632 --> 00:28:53,967
- What’s the matter
with you, Shep?

779
00:28:54,033 --> 00:28:55,268
- I’m tired, okay?

780
00:28:55,335 --> 00:28:56,568
- Ma!

781
00:28:56,635 --> 00:28:58,938
Ma, something’s
wrong with Shep!

782
00:28:59,005 --> 00:29:01,007
- Well, they shaved Shep,
and that’s all that’s left.

783
00:29:01,073 --> 00:29:02,875
TOMMY: What’s wrong with her,
Mr. Bald Eagle?

784
00:29:02,942 --> 00:29:04,443
- Please, it’s Bald.
TOMMY: What’s wrong?

785
00:29:04,510 --> 00:29:07,447
( Speaking native language )

786
00:29:09,048 --> 00:29:10,583
- Huh?

787
00:29:10,650 --> 00:29:14,887
- He said dog Shep not eat,
because she wants to go home.

788
00:29:14,953 --> 00:29:16,488
- To Jonathan?

789
00:29:16,555 --> 00:29:19,425
- I gave you the best years
of my life.

790
00:29:19,492 --> 00:29:23,830
( Speaking native language )

791
00:29:23,897 --> 00:29:25,265
Jonathan.

792
00:29:25,332 --> 00:29:30,203
- He say dog starve until die
if not go Jonathan.

793
00:29:30,270 --> 00:29:31,537
- Must drag butt
across driveway.

794
00:29:31,603 --> 00:29:33,105
- Thanks, Mr. Bald Eagle.

795
00:29:33,172 --> 00:29:35,842
Thanks.
- Lassie can get dressed now.

796
00:29:35,908 --> 00:29:37,043
- Goodbye, Red Wing.

797
00:29:37,110 --> 00:29:38,343
( Speaking native language )

798
00:29:38,410 --> 00:29:40,980
Ma, we’ve gotta get her
to Jonathan.

799
00:29:41,047 --> 00:29:43,348
Please say I can take her.
Please, Ma.

800
00:29:43,415 --> 00:29:45,718
- Maude?
- Well, I don’t know, dear.

801
00:29:45,785 --> 00:29:46,752
- Oh, hi.

802
00:29:46,820 --> 00:29:48,522
- How could you ever find him?

803
00:29:48,588 --> 00:29:51,023
- Shep could.
She knows where the claim is.

804
00:29:51,090 --> 00:29:52,625
- I’ll ride her there.

805
00:29:52,692 --> 00:29:54,560
- But you still
couldn’t go alone.

806
00:29:54,627 --> 00:29:56,595
- I’ll take him, Martha--
be glad to.

807
00:29:56,662 --> 00:29:57,897
- He’s hot for
the widow Martha.

808
00:29:57,963 --> 00:30:00,867
- Gee, thanks, Mr. Taylor.
- Aw.

809
00:30:00,933 --> 00:30:02,302
- Did you hear that, Shep?

810
00:30:02,368 --> 00:30:04,237
We’re going to take you back
to Jonathan.

811
00:30:04,303 --> 00:30:06,338
Now you’ve gotta eat
and get strong for the trip.

812
00:30:06,405 --> 00:30:08,642
- Look, I’ve got a lot of
other things on my mind.

813
00:30:08,708 --> 00:30:11,710
- Here, take some.
You’ve got to.

814
00:30:11,777 --> 00:30:14,913
- Come on, it’s milk of
human kindness, two percent.

815
00:30:18,250 --> 00:30:20,420
- He’s not mine, but I let him
live with me, heh.

816
00:30:23,823 --> 00:30:25,292
- Ma, look!
She’s eating!

817
00:30:25,358 --> 00:30:26,358
She’s gonna be all right!

818
00:30:26,425 --> 00:30:27,727
- Now, look,
if you keep this down,

819
00:30:27,793 --> 00:30:29,528
you can have some soda crackers
and watch "That Girl."

820
00:30:29,595 --> 00:30:30,863
( Shep barking )

821
00:30:30,930 --> 00:30:32,465
- ♪ The biggest sky
You ever saw ♪

822
00:30:32,532 --> 00:30:35,402
♪ Was in Seattle
Do-do-do-do-do ♪

823
00:30:36,602 --> 00:30:38,772
- He only lived
a couple of blocks away.

824
00:30:38,838 --> 00:30:40,707
- That’s not exactly
the incredible journey, is it?

825
00:30:40,773 --> 00:30:41,908
( Shep barking )

826
00:30:41,975 --> 00:30:43,042
- Shep, what is it?
Jonathan!

827
00:30:43,108 --> 00:30:45,512
- It’s a cabin,
you little turd.

828
00:30:45,578 --> 00:30:46,612
- Jonathan.

829
00:30:46,678 --> 00:30:47,747
JONATHAN:
Here, Tommy.

830
00:30:48,882 --> 00:30:50,015
- Jonathan, what’s wrong?

831
00:30:50,082 --> 00:30:51,083
- Hm?

832
00:30:51,150 --> 00:30:52,118
JONATHAN:
Fever.

833
00:30:53,185 --> 00:30:54,253
It hit me a week ago.

834
00:30:54,320 --> 00:30:56,122
- Match sure lit up the room.

835
00:30:56,188 --> 00:30:57,423
- The worst is past.

836
00:30:57,490 --> 00:30:58,958
- Quick, get him
a bowl of milk.

837
00:30:59,025 --> 00:31:02,428
- So weak, I couldn’t
get food any longer.

838
00:31:02,495 --> 00:31:04,597
- That’s what was
wrong with Shep.

839
00:31:04,663 --> 00:31:05,998
She knew.

840
00:31:06,065 --> 00:31:07,033
She wouldn’t eat.

841
00:31:07,100 --> 00:31:08,267
She made us bring her here.

842
00:31:08,333 --> 00:31:09,302
- No, you’re reaching, Tommy.

843
00:31:09,368 --> 00:31:11,237
- Ma’s right, Jonathan.

844
00:31:11,303 --> 00:31:14,207
Shep’s your dog
and nobody else’s.

845
00:31:14,273 --> 00:31:17,177
So, I’m giving her
back to you, right now.

846
00:31:17,243 --> 00:31:18,243
- Never mind the dog, Tommy.

847
00:31:18,310 --> 00:31:19,445
- Here’s the Sex Pistols.

848
00:31:19,512 --> 00:31:20,647
- Tommy,
you rustle up some wood.

849
00:31:20,713 --> 00:31:22,715
I’ll see about the food.

850
00:31:22,782 --> 00:31:24,217
- Well, we worked
the scam again.

851
00:31:24,283 --> 00:31:26,252
We get another free meal.

852
00:31:27,853 --> 00:31:29,155
- I’m all right, girl.

853
00:31:29,222 --> 00:31:30,790
- I just got into
a bad deer carcass.

854
00:31:30,857 --> 00:31:31,992
- And thanks.

855
00:31:33,092 --> 00:31:34,593
- Taylor--
- Pass the salt.

856
00:31:34,660 --> 00:31:37,197
- I guess you wonder how
our partnership’s been going.

857
00:31:37,263 --> 00:31:38,732
You were right.

858
00:31:38,798 --> 00:31:41,067
I’d have been a fool
to file that claim.

859
00:31:41,133 --> 00:31:42,635
The vein petered out
in a week.

860
00:31:42,702 --> 00:31:44,903
- That’s great.
You got any Mrs. Dash?

861
00:31:44,970 --> 00:31:46,605
- So, I just kept
working upstream.

862
00:31:46,672 --> 00:31:48,073
- Working?

863
00:31:48,140 --> 00:31:50,843
- Simple stuff,
hardly worth a man’s time.

864
00:31:50,910 --> 00:31:51,843
- How’s the Dinty Moore?

865
00:31:51,910 --> 00:31:53,212
- I’ll show you.

866
00:31:53,278 --> 00:31:55,915
Tommy, go on over there
by the shelf.

867
00:31:55,982 --> 00:31:57,250
- Grab the teapot
and come after me.

868
00:31:57,317 --> 00:31:58,918
JONATHAN:
Now slide that chest out.

869
00:32:00,520 --> 00:32:02,088
The board underneath
will lift up.

870
00:32:02,155 --> 00:32:03,790
- Get my magazines out.

871
00:32:06,258 --> 00:32:07,527
JONATHAN:
Take them out.

872
00:32:07,593 --> 00:32:10,095
- Then you go in there
and shut the top.

873
00:32:12,132 --> 00:32:13,332
- Is there a point to this?

874
00:32:17,970 --> 00:32:19,272
- Look inside, Tommy.

875
00:32:19,338 --> 00:32:20,640
- Do it!

876
00:32:20,707 --> 00:32:22,975
- What are you gonna do
with all this Bisquick?

877
00:32:24,943 --> 00:32:26,378
- How much is here, Jonathan?

878
00:32:26,445 --> 00:32:30,383
- Oh, not much;
only around about 6,000.

879
00:32:30,450 --> 00:32:32,818
- $6,000?
- No, drachmas.

880
00:32:32,885 --> 00:32:34,053
JONATHAN:
There’s plenty more to come.

881
00:32:34,120 --> 00:32:35,322
- 6,000.

882
00:32:35,388 --> 00:32:36,688
- That bass boat
I’ve been looking--

883
00:32:36,755 --> 00:32:38,057
- You say
that’s just from gravel?

884
00:32:38,123 --> 00:32:39,425
You still haven’t found
the main vein?

885
00:32:39,492 --> 00:32:41,527
- No, but it’s up there.

886
00:32:41,593 --> 00:32:44,663
I aim to just work upstream
until I find it;

887
00:32:44,730 --> 00:32:47,400
and I will, as sure as
your name is Lin Taylor.

888
00:32:47,467 --> 00:32:48,835
- My name is Tom Wopat.

889
00:32:48,902 --> 00:32:50,737
- Jonathan, do you realize
what this means?

890
00:32:50,803 --> 00:32:52,905
- It means we’re rich,
all of us.

891
00:32:52,972 --> 00:32:54,807
- Sure, we are, but that’s not
what I’m talking about.

892
00:32:54,873 --> 00:32:56,275
You need help.

893
00:32:56,342 --> 00:32:58,812
Now, look, I’m starting back
to town tomorrow.

894
00:32:58,878 --> 00:33:01,347
I’ll take this gold and deposit
it in all our names.

895
00:33:01,413 --> 00:33:03,548
It’ll be safe there,
and I’ll be back.

896
00:33:03,615 --> 00:33:05,050
Why, with proper equipment,

897
00:33:05,117 --> 00:33:06,818
we can take that gold out
ten times as fast.

898
00:33:06,885 --> 00:33:08,587
- Sit down and eat your stew.

899
00:33:11,257 --> 00:33:14,260
- All my life I’ve waited
for something like this,

900
00:33:14,327 --> 00:33:16,362
and I’m not taking any chance
on losing it now.

901
00:33:16,428 --> 00:33:18,630
- Grated cheese?

902
00:33:19,765 --> 00:33:22,768
- Mount Rushmore
before it was built.

903
00:33:24,070 --> 00:33:25,938
- I just love these
new age type nature tapes.

904
00:33:26,005 --> 00:33:27,440
They’re so relaxing.

905
00:33:29,375 --> 00:33:31,810
- And a rapid runs through it.

906
00:33:31,877 --> 00:33:34,180
- We’ll run the inlet channel
across there

907
00:33:34,247 --> 00:33:35,848
and build a smooth spot
below the rapids.

908
00:33:35,915 --> 00:33:37,350
- I love movies

909
00:33:37,417 --> 00:33:39,885
where they slowly
assemble the plot like this.

910
00:33:39,952 --> 00:33:42,020
- Now, what did we build again,
Steve?

911
00:33:42,088 --> 00:33:43,255
- Let her go, Tommy!

912
00:33:43,322 --> 00:33:45,458
- Well, look,
Shep is Tommy’s supervisor.

913
00:33:45,525 --> 00:33:46,458
- Hm.

914
00:33:47,660 --> 00:33:48,995
- We invented
the first water flume!

915
00:33:49,062 --> 00:33:50,062
It’s fun!

916
00:33:50,128 --> 00:33:51,663
- Ha, ha, ha.
- Ha!

917
00:33:51,730 --> 00:33:54,167
- We’ll see one day’s take
from this baby, Taylor.

918
00:33:54,233 --> 00:33:55,367
Just wait.

919
00:33:58,570 --> 00:34:00,038
- How long do I have to wait?

920
00:34:02,475 --> 00:34:05,678
- First thing I’m gonna do
is buy me a montage, yeah!

921
00:34:05,745 --> 00:34:06,678
- Yee-hoo!

922
00:34:06,745 --> 00:34:07,813
- This bites.

923
00:34:07,880 --> 00:34:09,082
- Snausages!

924
00:34:10,850 --> 00:34:12,685
- What is it
we’re doing again?

925
00:34:12,752 --> 00:34:14,287
- Kenny Rogers as Moses.

926
00:34:17,190 --> 00:34:18,792
- Industry!

927
00:34:20,560 --> 00:34:22,027
- Any Snausages in there?

928
00:34:23,195 --> 00:34:25,130
- Come on, work!

929
00:34:25,197 --> 00:34:28,300
( Music playing )

930
00:34:29,635 --> 00:34:32,272
- Rutherford B. Hayes
strikes it rich!

931
00:34:32,338 --> 00:34:33,272
- Whew!

932
00:34:34,507 --> 00:34:35,708
- Santon!

933
00:34:37,843 --> 00:34:39,478
- I feel damp all over.

934
00:34:42,080 --> 00:34:43,315
- It’s fun!

935
00:34:43,382 --> 00:34:45,217
- The world is dying.

936
00:34:45,283 --> 00:34:47,520
- What? What?

937
00:34:47,587 --> 00:34:50,523
( Music playing )

938
00:34:52,525 --> 00:34:55,495
- Well, looks like the montage
finally blew over.

939
00:34:55,562 --> 00:34:58,865
( Theme music playing )

940
00:35:01,133 --> 00:35:03,568
- Hey, it’s Juan Valdez.

941
00:35:05,337 --> 00:35:06,672
- Hey, it’s someone else.

942
00:35:06,738 --> 00:35:09,308
- The Denver Pyle look
was very popular this year.

943
00:35:09,375 --> 00:35:12,778
- We’re being ransacked
by Tom Bombadil.

944
00:35:12,845 --> 00:35:14,480
( Humming "bombadil" )

945
00:35:14,547 --> 00:35:17,015
- Finally,
sweet Metamucil.

946
00:35:18,683 --> 00:35:21,253
There’s the pretty underthings.
( CHUCKLING )

947
00:35:21,320 --> 00:35:22,788
- Go get it!

948
00:35:22,855 --> 00:35:24,490
Go get what you put
in that saddlebag!

949
00:35:24,557 --> 00:35:26,225
- Solzhenitsyn?
- Oh.

950
00:35:26,292 --> 00:35:27,960
TAYLOR: For days,
you’ve been watching us,

951
00:35:28,027 --> 00:35:29,528
but you didn’t fool me.

952
00:35:29,595 --> 00:35:31,663
I knew you were out there.

953
00:35:31,730 --> 00:35:33,398
You heard what I said. Move!

954
00:35:33,465 --> 00:35:34,800
- Come on, Kringle!

955
00:35:36,202 --> 00:35:38,103
- Go on. Go get
what you put in there.

956
00:35:38,170 --> 00:35:41,040
- Taylor!
What’s going on here?

957
00:35:41,107 --> 00:35:42,040
- You keep out of this,
Jonathan.

958
00:35:42,107 --> 00:35:43,042
I know what I’m doing.

959
00:35:43,108 --> 00:35:44,377
- Don’t shoot my doppelganger.

960
00:35:44,443 --> 00:35:46,078
- Will you get it,
or don’t you?

961
00:35:46,145 --> 00:35:48,413
- You crazy fool!
I ought to lay you low!

962
00:35:48,480 --> 00:35:50,082
- Make him show you
what he put in that saddlebag.

963
00:35:50,148 --> 00:35:52,685
- I took something
from there for this house.

964
00:35:52,752 --> 00:35:55,522
- He’s lying.
Look for yourself.

965
00:35:55,588 --> 00:35:57,690
- No, Taylor, you look.

966
00:35:57,757 --> 00:35:59,525
- No, you look.
- No, you look.

967
00:35:59,592 --> 00:36:00,693
- You first.

968
00:36:00,760 --> 00:36:01,693
- Ah.

969
00:36:04,563 --> 00:36:05,497
- Hm?

970
00:36:06,565 --> 00:36:07,900
- He’s Gideon!

971
00:36:09,268 --> 00:36:11,670
- Huh, he was already
sent out, huh?

972
00:36:13,907 --> 00:36:16,108
- But he was inside,
searching around, snooping.

973
00:36:17,710 --> 00:36:18,710
- For food!

974
00:36:20,445 --> 00:36:23,950
I would’ve prepared a table
in the presence of my friends.

975
00:36:24,017 --> 00:36:25,450
- Oh, you’re so holy.

976
00:36:25,517 --> 00:36:27,820
- Oh, my mistake.

977
00:36:27,887 --> 00:36:29,655
- This is Pilot Pete, Taylor.
- Pile On Pete?

978
00:36:29,722 --> 00:36:31,090
- If you’d been long
in these hills,

979
00:36:31,157 --> 00:36:33,325
you’d never have made
that mistake.

980
00:36:33,392 --> 00:36:34,660
Don’t make it again.

981
00:36:35,962 --> 00:36:37,330
Come on, Pete.

982
00:36:37,397 --> 00:36:39,232
- Pile on.
- Let’s see about that supper.

983
00:36:41,567 --> 00:36:43,368
- You a regular parson,
Mr. Pete?

984
00:36:43,435 --> 00:36:45,003
- Regular?
- No, son.

985
00:36:45,070 --> 00:36:46,905
Reckon I’m as irregular
as they come.

986
00:36:46,972 --> 00:36:48,273
- Eww!

987
00:36:48,340 --> 00:36:50,542
- Gotta have roots
to be a regular parson.

988
00:36:50,610 --> 00:36:52,010
- I’m a heretic.

989
00:36:52,077 --> 00:36:54,047
- Me, I ain’t got the roots
of a tumbleweed.

990
00:36:54,113 --> 00:36:56,348
Two things I love
above all else.

991
00:36:56,415 --> 00:36:57,950
- Larry and Balki.

992
00:36:58,017 --> 00:37:00,620
- The Lord’s mountains
and the Lord’s sinners.

993
00:37:00,687 --> 00:37:04,923
So, I spend my time in one
looking for the other,

994
00:37:04,990 --> 00:37:06,725
and I sure find them.

995
00:37:06,792 --> 00:37:09,428
- I’m a lawn gnome
in the summertime!

996
00:37:10,495 --> 00:37:11,863
- What? I’m right here.

997
00:37:11,930 --> 00:37:14,367
You better leave Shep alone.

998
00:37:14,433 --> 00:37:16,968
She never makes up
to strangers.

999
00:37:17,035 --> 00:37:18,437
- She don’t?

1000
00:37:18,503 --> 00:37:21,473
Here, here, lady!
Here, lady!

1001
00:37:21,540 --> 00:37:23,042
( Bot growling )

1002
00:37:23,108 --> 00:37:25,077
- Well, you’ve got her wrong,
Brother Taylor.

1003
00:37:25,143 --> 00:37:30,082
Thou sawest the beast that was
and is not, Revelation 17:8.

1004
00:37:30,148 --> 00:37:31,850
Why, there’s plenty of love
in this dog

1005
00:37:31,917 --> 00:37:33,385
for them that loves her.

1006
00:37:33,452 --> 00:37:35,287
Ain’t there, Shep?

1007
00:37:35,353 --> 00:37:37,188
Or maybe she just
likes my stew.

1008
00:37:37,255 --> 00:37:39,625
- Book of me,
chapter 4, verse 12.

1009
00:37:39,692 --> 00:37:41,893
- Heh!
- Pete--

1010
00:37:41,960 --> 00:37:43,162
- Give me my underthings back.

1011
00:37:43,228 --> 00:37:44,197
- This is from all of us.

1012
00:37:44,263 --> 00:37:45,732
- It’s a carrot.

1013
00:37:45,798 --> 00:37:50,535
- Well, in His name,
I thank you, Jonathan, Tommy,

1014
00:37:50,602 --> 00:37:54,207
Mr. Taylor;
and you, too, lady fair.

1015
00:37:54,273 --> 00:37:56,175
- The name’s Shep.
Remember it!

1016
00:37:56,242 --> 00:37:58,110
- The Lord be with you
in all your labors.

1017
00:37:58,177 --> 00:37:59,578
- And also with you.

1018
00:37:59,645 --> 00:38:02,348
- Lay not up for yourselves
treasures on earth,

1019
00:38:02,415 --> 00:38:05,150
but lay up for yourselves
treasures in heaven.

1020
00:38:05,217 --> 00:38:07,252
- In an interest bearing CD.

1021
00:38:07,320 --> 00:38:11,123
- Matthew 6:19.
- Got the half.

1022
00:38:11,190 --> 00:38:12,425
That’s a little preacher joke.

1023
00:38:12,492 --> 00:38:14,560
Well, I’m off
to the King Lear convention!

1024
00:38:14,627 --> 00:38:15,962
- Get up, Gabriel!

1025
00:38:16,028 --> 00:38:17,930
- So, this guy comes in,
stops the plot cold and leaves.

1026
00:38:17,997 --> 00:38:19,098
- Yep.
- Bye, Mr. Pete.

1027
00:38:19,165 --> 00:38:20,498
- Goodbye, Pete.
- Goodbye.

1028
00:38:20,565 --> 00:38:23,602
♪ Work for the night
’tis come ♪

1029
00:38:23,668 --> 00:38:25,570
- ♪ Work for the union label ♪

1030
00:38:25,637 --> 00:38:28,273
- ♪ Work through
The morning on ♪

1031
00:38:28,340 --> 00:38:30,542
- All right, new rule.
No crusty old evangelists.

1032
00:38:30,608 --> 00:38:34,080
PETE: ♪ Work while the dew
is sparkling ♪

1033
00:38:34,147 --> 00:38:35,982
- Tommy, you go down to work.
- Wook?

1034
00:38:36,048 --> 00:38:36,982
- I’ll be along.

1035
00:38:39,318 --> 00:38:42,153
- Do you mind explaining
yourself, please?

1036
00:38:42,220 --> 00:38:44,957
- Well? You gave him dust.

1037
00:38:45,023 --> 00:38:47,292
That’s a dead giveaway
we struck pay dirt.

1038
00:38:47,360 --> 00:38:48,627
The old fool talks--
- He won’t.

1039
00:38:48,693 --> 00:38:49,995
- I’ve seen to that.

1040
00:38:50,062 --> 00:38:51,930
- Pete knows the law
of these hills, Taylor,

1041
00:38:51,997 --> 00:38:53,765
and he lives by it.

1042
00:38:53,832 --> 00:38:55,400
You could try doing the same.

1043
00:38:55,467 --> 00:38:56,702
- Yeah, try piling on once.

1044
00:38:56,768 --> 00:38:59,272
- Until you do,
I’m taking that gun.

1045
00:38:59,338 --> 00:39:01,040
- I keep my own gun.

1046
00:39:01,107 --> 00:39:02,908
- You’re edgy, Taylor,

1047
00:39:02,975 --> 00:39:06,645
and getting worse every time
more gold is under that floor.

1048
00:39:06,712 --> 00:39:09,915
It ain’t just being scared
someone will steal it, neither.

1049
00:39:09,982 --> 00:39:11,517
It’s having to split
it with Martha and me.

1050
00:39:11,583 --> 00:39:13,052
- You’re crazy.

1051
00:39:13,118 --> 00:39:15,788
- "Only a quarter,"
you keep thinking to yourself.

1052
00:39:15,855 --> 00:39:20,425
"I only get a quarter of it,
and Jonathan gets half."

1053
00:39:20,492 --> 00:39:22,995
That keeps
eatin’ at you, Taylor.

1054
00:39:23,062 --> 00:39:25,530
I’ve been watching you,
and I know.

1055
00:39:25,597 --> 00:39:27,633
That’s why I’m
taking that gun.

1056
00:39:27,700 --> 00:39:29,568
- Let’s not fight
in front of the collie.

1057
00:39:29,635 --> 00:39:31,637
- No, you won’t do that.

1058
00:39:31,703 --> 00:39:33,472
There’s two reasons
why you won’t shoot me, Taylor.

1059
00:39:33,538 --> 00:39:35,007
- Three!

1060
00:39:35,073 --> 00:39:37,477
- The first, you won’t find
that vein without me;

1061
00:39:37,543 --> 00:39:42,113
and the other,
the other is just a hunch

1062
00:39:42,180 --> 00:39:43,982
that you haven’t got the guts.

1063
00:39:44,048 --> 00:39:44,983
- And the third?

1064
00:39:45,050 --> 00:39:46,952
Oh, I forgot where I was.

1065
00:39:47,018 --> 00:39:50,688
- Uh, would this be a bad time
to bring up Snausages?

1066
00:39:53,658 --> 00:39:56,828
- I was born in the house
my father built.

1067
00:39:58,563 --> 00:40:01,000
- Oh, man, I’m turning into
Victor French!

1068
00:40:02,735 --> 00:40:04,870
- It snowed.
Should we be working?

1069
00:40:06,372 --> 00:40:08,407
- There’s a lot of closet space
in this cabin, huh?

1070
00:40:10,142 --> 00:40:12,812
- Hey, his pajamas are just like
his clothes, heh, heh.

1071
00:40:15,180 --> 00:40:17,382
- Ah, the violins
are urging him on.

1072
00:40:17,450 --> 00:40:18,983
- The pistol.

1073
00:40:19,050 --> 00:40:21,987
- Well, he’s got
child-bearing hips.

1074
00:40:22,053 --> 00:40:25,457
- Jonathan, Jonathan,
can I get a glass of water?

1075
00:40:26,758 --> 00:40:30,695
- Oh, oh, yes.

1076
00:40:30,762 --> 00:40:35,700
Oh, I just have to
touch that hair.

1077
00:40:35,767 --> 00:40:38,103
Oh, soft.

1078
00:40:38,170 --> 00:40:40,338
- He’s gonna kipe
his copy of "The Firm."

1079
00:40:42,440 --> 00:40:45,110
- Ooh, ooh, ooh.

1080
00:40:46,712 --> 00:40:48,413
( SHEP GROWLING )
- No, no, cut, cut!

1081
00:40:48,480 --> 00:40:49,748
No, Lassie, it’s more of a--

1082
00:40:49,815 --> 00:40:51,850
( bot imitating
fierce growling )

1083
00:40:53,918 --> 00:40:56,655
( SHEP GROWLING )
- I said put it back!

1084
00:40:58,857 --> 00:41:02,995
- Hmph, well, okay,
I think I can stand up,

1085
00:41:03,062 --> 00:41:08,367
and walk over here,
and sidle over to the door,

1086
00:41:08,433 --> 00:41:10,468
open it up, and run!

1087
00:41:13,038 --> 00:41:16,208
- Works every time.
Ha, ha, ha!

1088
00:41:16,275 --> 00:41:17,408
- Heh, heh, now we rock.

1089
00:41:17,475 --> 00:41:18,877
- Good girl.

1090
00:41:18,943 --> 00:41:19,878
- Hey, my reward.

1091
00:41:19,945 --> 00:41:21,113
Squeaky newspaper,
Gaines-Burger,

1092
00:41:21,180 --> 00:41:22,982
Snausage,
anything, huh?

1093
00:41:24,750 --> 00:41:26,818
- Well, he didn’t see
the good grooming short, did he?

1094
00:41:26,885 --> 00:41:27,853
- Don’t think so.

1095
00:41:29,922 --> 00:41:31,423
- Look, Big Foot
putting on a blazer.

1096
00:41:31,490 --> 00:41:32,423
- Huh.

1097
00:41:35,227 --> 00:41:39,097
- And so the little fellow
fled the vicious cabin.

1098
00:41:39,163 --> 00:41:41,533
- Stallone in "Cliffhanger."

1099
00:41:41,600 --> 00:41:43,002
- Ah, he’s gotta be careful,

1100
00:41:43,068 --> 00:41:45,070
or he might fall right into
that matte painting.

1101
00:41:45,137 --> 00:41:46,305
- Oh, that’s dangerous.

1102
00:41:46,372 --> 00:41:48,907
( Suspenseful music )

1103
00:41:48,973 --> 00:41:52,210
- Well, this is far enough.
I guess I can go here.

1104
00:41:54,380 --> 00:41:55,780
- Heh, I guess
that convenience store

1105
00:41:55,847 --> 00:41:56,915
isn’t so convenient.

1106
00:41:56,982 --> 00:41:58,083
Ha, ha.

1107
00:41:59,885 --> 00:42:02,822
- Note to myself: "Next time,
go the way you know."

1108
00:42:02,888 --> 00:42:06,392
( Suspenseful music )

1109
00:42:06,458 --> 00:42:07,392
- Huh?

1110
00:42:07,458 --> 00:42:09,728
- Hey! Ride ’em.

1111
00:42:09,795 --> 00:42:12,732
( Suspenseful music )

1112
00:42:14,098 --> 00:42:18,370
- Oh, man, is it tough
to get to the fourth tee!

1113
00:42:18,437 --> 00:42:20,005
- Ooh.

1114
00:42:20,072 --> 00:42:23,508
( Suspenseful music )

1115
00:42:25,210 --> 00:42:27,012
- Leon Trotsky
in the action adventure

1116
00:42:27,078 --> 00:42:29,080
you thought
he’d never make.

1117
00:42:29,147 --> 00:42:32,050
( Suspenseful music )

1118
00:42:32,117 --> 00:42:34,320
- Well, back to the cabin.

1119
00:42:36,822 --> 00:42:39,057
- Oh, oh, I thought
that was a matte painting.

1120
00:42:39,123 --> 00:42:40,758
Good thing I didn’t fall.

1121
00:42:42,060 --> 00:42:44,597
- All right, about time
for that Nut Goodie.

1122
00:42:44,663 --> 00:42:46,165
- Heh, heh, heh, heh.

1123
00:42:48,733 --> 00:42:51,703
- Ow, ow, ow, ow, ow.

1124
00:42:51,770 --> 00:42:53,005
Why am I doing this?

1125
00:42:53,072 --> 00:42:55,373
- This old mountain.

1126
00:42:57,175 --> 00:43:00,145
( Theme music playing )

1127
00:43:03,115 --> 00:43:05,217
- With an ice pick
through the skull,

1128
00:43:05,283 --> 00:43:08,887
thus bringing to a brutal end
the passionate life of one

1129
00:43:08,953 --> 00:43:13,192
Lev Davidovich Bronshtein,
Leon Trotsky.

1130
00:43:13,258 --> 00:43:14,192
Thank you.

1131
00:43:14,258 --> 00:43:15,593
- Oh, wow!

1132
00:43:15,660 --> 00:43:17,428
- Gypsy,
that was terrific!

1133
00:43:17,495 --> 00:43:18,763
- Oh, thank you, guys.
- It was riveting!

1134
00:43:18,830 --> 00:43:19,832
- Oh, thank you.
- Oh, hi, everybody.

1135
00:43:19,898 --> 00:43:21,633
Welcome to
the Satellite of Love.

1136
00:43:21,700 --> 00:43:22,868
We’re just finishing up

1137
00:43:22,935 --> 00:43:24,135
this week’s
term paper assignment,

1138
00:43:24,202 --> 00:43:26,538
the topic being
"Famous Figures in History

1139
00:43:26,605 --> 00:43:29,675
that Look Like the Cantankerous
49’ers in Today’s Film."

1140
00:43:29,742 --> 00:43:31,777
I did one on Aristotle.
- It’s excellent, by the way.

1141
00:43:31,843 --> 00:43:34,780
- Tom Servo did a hilarious
send-up of the Smith Brothers.

1142
00:43:34,847 --> 00:43:36,248
- You should’ve been here.
I did both parts.

1143
00:43:36,315 --> 00:43:37,783
- Yeah-- and now
that we’re all done,

1144
00:43:37,850 --> 00:43:39,718
it’s time for
Ovaltine and popcorn.

1145
00:43:39,785 --> 00:43:40,785
- Oh, I just love Ovaltine.

1146
00:43:40,852 --> 00:43:41,920
- Hey, hey, what about me?

1147
00:43:41,987 --> 00:43:42,988
Hey, what am I,
burnt toast here?

1148
00:43:43,055 --> 00:43:45,057
- Oh, I’m sorry, Crow.

1149
00:43:45,123 --> 00:43:46,992
And now Crow T. Robot.

1150
00:43:47,058 --> 00:43:49,193
- Uh, uh, put my study aid up.
- What?

1151
00:43:49,260 --> 00:43:50,428
- My study aid.
It fell down.

1152
00:43:50,495 --> 00:43:51,697
Put that up.
- Oh, okay, all right.

1153
00:43:51,763 --> 00:43:54,065
- Oh, and, uh, Cambot,
cue that music, please!

1154
00:43:54,132 --> 00:43:57,335
( CLEARING THROAT )
Rutherford B. Hayes.

1155
00:43:57,402 --> 00:44:00,538
( CLEARING THROAT )
Rutherford B. Hayes was born

1156
00:44:00,605 --> 00:44:03,242
humbly to his own son,
Rutherford B. Hayes, Jr.,

1157
00:44:03,308 --> 00:44:05,877
in Delaware,
while it was still Ohio,

1158
00:44:05,943 --> 00:44:08,013
sometime after
the French Revolution.

1159
00:44:08,080 --> 00:44:10,415
Rejecting a career
as a professional speller,

1160
00:44:10,482 --> 00:44:12,583
he was admitted to the bar
in 1845,

1161
00:44:12,650 --> 00:44:14,953
though he did not drink
lustfully from it.

1162
00:44:15,020 --> 00:44:16,322
- Uh-huh.

1163
00:44:16,388 --> 00:44:17,757
- Serving heroically
in the Civil War,

1164
00:44:17,823 --> 00:44:19,190
Hayes admitted later
that it was in the army

1165
00:44:19,257 --> 00:44:21,593
he first tasted human flesh.

1166
00:44:21,660 --> 00:44:25,730
In 1876, Hayes beat Bill Tilden
in a three-set quarter final

1167
00:44:25,797 --> 00:44:26,865
at Flushing Meadows,

1168
00:44:26,932 --> 00:44:28,300
which caused
the electrical college

1169
00:44:28,367 --> 00:44:31,103
to declare him
President of the United States.

1170
00:44:31,170 --> 00:44:34,573
- Uh, Crow, you just kind of
threw this together, didn’t you?

1171
00:44:34,640 --> 00:44:36,942
- Please, Joel, I’m quoting
from the World Book,

1172
00:44:37,008 --> 00:44:38,177
chapter H.

1173
00:44:38,243 --> 00:44:39,612
- Huh.

1174
00:44:39,678 --> 00:44:41,313
- ( CLEARING THROAT )
Here are a few of the highlights

1175
00:44:41,380 --> 00:44:43,315
of the administration
of Rutherford B. Hayes.

1176
00:44:43,382 --> 00:44:44,315
- Mm-hm.

1177
00:44:44,383 --> 00:44:46,685
- In 1877,
reconstruction ended,

1178
00:44:46,752 --> 00:44:49,187
and Jaques Derrida was named
Secretary of Linguistics,

1179
00:44:49,253 --> 00:44:52,858
and the era of deconstruction
began and continues to this day.

1180
00:44:52,925 --> 00:44:54,158
- I just don’t think that--

1181
00:44:54,225 --> 00:44:56,162
- Thomas Edison invented
the pornograph,

1182
00:44:56,228 --> 00:44:57,662
beginning the age
of pornography.

1183
00:44:57,730 --> 00:44:59,598
President Hayes
then passed the Hayes Act,

1184
00:44:59,665 --> 00:45:00,732
started the Hayes Office,

1185
00:45:00,798 --> 00:45:02,367
won fame
as an American lyric tenor,

1186
00:45:02,433 --> 00:45:05,403
and was named Archbishop
of New York in 1919.

1187
00:45:05,470 --> 00:45:06,538
- I think he’s lying!

1188
00:45:06,605 --> 00:45:07,638
- No, I’m not.

1189
00:45:07,705 --> 00:45:08,973
( Clearing throat )

1190
00:45:09,040 --> 00:45:11,610
After he retired,
he founded the original ZZ Top,

1191
00:45:11,677 --> 00:45:12,845
with James Garfield--

1192
00:45:12,912 --> 00:45:14,345
- Oh, yeah.
- --and Chester Alan Arthur,

1193
00:45:14,413 --> 00:45:16,582
shocked the world
with a publicity stunt,

1194
00:45:16,648 --> 00:45:19,250
when, on a bet,
he made a tent

1195
00:45:19,317 --> 00:45:20,718
out of the underwear
of William Howard Taft--

1196
00:45:20,785 --> 00:45:21,787
- Oh, come on!

1197
00:45:21,853 --> 00:45:22,888
- --and lived inside
for a full year.

1198
00:45:22,955 --> 00:45:24,255
Well, he did.
- Yeah.

1199
00:45:24,322 --> 00:45:25,723
- And in later years,
Hayes retired from the stage

1200
00:45:25,790 --> 00:45:28,427
and did a series of memorable
character parts in Hollywood.

1201
00:45:28,493 --> 00:45:31,130
Who can forget the time he was
slapped by Jacqueline Bisset

1202
00:45:31,197 --> 00:45:33,665
in the 1971 Universal movie,
"Airport"?

1203
00:45:33,732 --> 00:45:35,200
( LAUGHING )
- Who was that?

1204
00:45:35,267 --> 00:45:37,535
- And then after inventing
ringworm, Hayes died.

1205
00:45:37,602 --> 00:45:40,705
His last words were,
"I have only one life to live;

1206
00:45:40,772 --> 00:45:43,775
let me live it as a blond."
The end.

1207
00:45:45,243 --> 00:45:46,845
- Whew.
- Oh, and his blood type was AB.

1208
00:45:46,912 --> 00:45:48,080
The end.

1209
00:45:48,147 --> 00:45:49,548
- Sad what the mind can do,
isn’t it?

1210
00:45:49,615 --> 00:45:51,483
- Yeah, I’m gonna have to
hold you back a year, Crow.

1211
00:45:51,550 --> 00:45:53,185
- Cool.
Can I have my same desk?

1212
00:45:53,252 --> 00:45:56,488
( Alarm going off )
( Shouting )

1213
00:45:56,555 --> 00:45:58,890
- Rutherford B. Hayes.

1214
00:46:05,563 --> 00:46:06,798
- I want you to take this
to your Ma.

1215
00:46:06,865 --> 00:46:08,633
- Rutherford Hayes
all through my brain!

1216
00:46:08,700 --> 00:46:10,568
- Tell her I said we’re
going to file it right away.

1217
00:46:10,635 --> 00:46:13,372
It’s a description of the claim
made out in all our names--

1218
00:46:13,438 --> 00:46:14,873
hers, Taylor’s, and mine.

1219
00:46:14,940 --> 00:46:16,475
- Is there anything wrong,
Jonathan?

1220
00:46:16,542 --> 00:46:19,443
- No, this is just in case
someone gets the bright idea

1221
00:46:19,512 --> 00:46:20,978
to file it only
in their own name.

1222
00:46:21,045 --> 00:46:21,980
( Bots whispering )

1223
00:46:22,047 --> 00:46:23,215
- Oh, Mr. Taylor.

1224
00:46:23,282 --> 00:46:25,117
- But remember,
whatever happens,

1225
00:46:25,183 --> 00:46:28,920
not a word to anyone
about my doubt, even your ma.

1226
00:46:28,987 --> 00:46:30,322
Promise?

1227
00:46:30,388 --> 00:46:32,290
Now, come on,
hightail it.

1228
00:46:32,357 --> 00:46:33,792
Hurry up.

1229
00:46:33,858 --> 00:46:36,462
- Yeah, and if you run into
Pile On Pete, don’t look at him.

1230
00:46:38,530 --> 00:46:39,598
- Tell your ma not to worry.

1231
00:46:39,665 --> 00:46:42,000
- I will.
Bye, Jonathan.

1232
00:46:42,067 --> 00:46:45,737
- Oh, oh, and bring me back
some OJ and a Cosmo magazine.

1233
00:46:45,803 --> 00:46:48,807
- Shep, where are you?

1234
00:46:48,873 --> 00:46:50,875
Shep, what is this?

1235
00:46:50,942 --> 00:46:52,945
- I ate some gold.
- What ails you?

1236
00:46:53,012 --> 00:46:55,413
You know, between us, we’ve
got to take care of that boy.

1237
00:46:55,480 --> 00:46:57,682
He don’t know these trails
like we do.

1238
00:46:57,748 --> 00:47:00,452
Oh, it ain’t for good
this time.

1239
00:47:00,518 --> 00:47:03,055
You’re coming back.
Now go on, git.

1240
00:47:03,122 --> 00:47:05,857
- Yes, master,
I’m your total slave.

1241
00:47:05,923 --> 00:47:09,662
( Shep barking )

1242
00:47:09,728 --> 00:47:11,863
- There goes one stupid dog.

1243
00:47:11,930 --> 00:47:13,098
- Jonathan!

1244
00:47:15,333 --> 00:47:16,468
Jonathan...

1245
00:47:16,535 --> 00:47:17,903
- I set a trap for you and-- oh.

1246
00:47:17,970 --> 00:47:20,138
Where are they going?
- I sent Tommy home.

1247
00:47:20,205 --> 00:47:22,473
The kid’s getting lonesome
for his ma.

1248
00:47:22,540 --> 00:47:24,108
What’s all the excitement?

1249
00:47:24,175 --> 00:47:26,010
- Jonathan, that vein
we’ve been looking for--

1250
00:47:26,077 --> 00:47:27,345
if it was up there,
what would it look like?

1251
00:47:27,412 --> 00:47:29,180
- Well, uh--
- No, I’ll tell you.

1252
00:47:29,247 --> 00:47:30,915
The sheer face
of the cliff,

1253
00:47:30,982 --> 00:47:33,018
white cords,
the whitest you ever saw;

1254
00:47:33,085 --> 00:47:34,653
and in it,
jagged like lightning,

1255
00:47:34,720 --> 00:47:36,422
a vein of pure gold.

1256
00:47:36,488 --> 00:47:38,257
Gold, Jonathan, that wide.
It’s up there.

1257
00:47:38,323 --> 00:47:39,457
So help me, I found it.

1258
00:47:39,523 --> 00:47:40,492
It’s up there
waiting for us!

1259
00:47:40,558 --> 00:47:41,660
- Where?

1260
00:47:41,727 --> 00:47:43,428
- At the head of the canyon,
way up.

1261
00:47:43,495 --> 00:47:44,597
- Well, let’s go.
- It’s where I’m gonna
kill you.

1262
00:47:44,663 --> 00:47:45,930
Ooh, I can never
keep a secret.

1263
00:47:45,997 --> 00:47:48,800
( Shep barking )

1264
00:47:48,867 --> 00:47:52,037
- How do you work
this crazy thing?

1265
00:47:52,103 --> 00:47:54,272
( Suspenseful music )

1266
00:47:54,338 --> 00:47:56,508
- Come on. How stupid
are you feeling today?

1267
00:47:58,843 --> 00:47:59,778
( Shep barking )

1268
00:47:59,845 --> 00:48:01,012
- The British are coming!

1269
00:48:01,078 --> 00:48:02,113
Get away from me, dog.
The British are coming!

1270
00:48:02,180 --> 00:48:04,048
Would you just get away?
The--

1271
00:48:05,950 --> 00:48:07,718
- It’s the Von Trapp
family prospectors.

1272
00:48:09,320 --> 00:48:11,923
- Go on, Shep!
Go on back!

1273
00:48:11,990 --> 00:48:13,092
Home, girl!

1274
00:48:13,158 --> 00:48:14,458
- Wait a minute!
- Go back!

1275
00:48:14,525 --> 00:48:15,760
- What? I’m a dog!

1276
00:48:15,827 --> 00:48:19,263
I can’t process
this much information at once!

1277
00:48:19,330 --> 00:48:21,500
- Look, I’m 100 years old.
Where is this thing?

1278
00:48:21,567 --> 00:48:23,002
- You were sure right
about the mountain goats.

1279
00:48:23,068 --> 00:48:24,903
- Yeah, you said it.

1280
00:48:31,910 --> 00:48:33,545
- Now let’s see
if these two sequences

1281
00:48:33,612 --> 00:48:35,613
relate to each other.

1282
00:48:35,680 --> 00:48:37,315
- Go ahead.
- Make my day.

1283
00:48:43,288 --> 00:48:45,123
- Ooh, your hands
are really soft.

1284
00:48:45,190 --> 00:48:46,692
( Shep barking )

1285
00:48:46,758 --> 00:48:49,393
- Come on,
I need to use the bathroom!

1286
00:48:49,460 --> 00:48:50,495
Is this the right place?

1287
00:48:57,135 --> 00:49:00,072
- Oh, he didn’t make my bed.

1288
00:49:05,343 --> 00:49:06,545
Typical.

1289
00:49:15,487 --> 00:49:17,388
You know, there’s a shuttle bus
that goes here.

1290
00:49:17,455 --> 00:49:18,690
- Oh.

1291
00:49:20,725 --> 00:49:22,627
- Gotta get that stupid
bathroom key.

1292
00:49:25,030 --> 00:49:28,333
- Man, he’s really making him
work for his death.

1293
00:49:28,400 --> 00:49:29,835
- Heh, heh, heh.

1294
00:49:29,902 --> 00:49:31,603
- It’s the ledge just beyond.

1295
00:49:31,670 --> 00:49:34,873
( Suspenseful music )

1296
00:49:38,110 --> 00:49:39,043
- Let’s go.

1297
00:49:41,947 --> 00:49:42,913
- Come on over here.

1298
00:49:42,980 --> 00:49:43,948
This is where
I put the loose rock

1299
00:49:44,015 --> 00:49:45,283
for you to step on.

1300
00:49:49,353 --> 00:49:55,193
- Yes, yes, yes, yes,
yes, yes, oh, yes, yes.

1301
00:49:55,260 --> 00:49:56,928
- Oh, I failed
to take into account

1302
00:49:56,995 --> 00:49:59,832
that he only weighs
63 pounds.

1303
00:49:59,898 --> 00:50:00,865
- Well, where is it?

1304
00:50:00,932 --> 00:50:02,100
- Son of a gun...
You know what?

1305
00:50:02,167 --> 00:50:04,068
It’s back a ways.

1306
00:50:06,270 --> 00:50:08,005
( SNIFFING )
- Obsession.

1307
00:50:10,775 --> 00:50:12,610
- Taylor!

1308
00:50:12,677 --> 00:50:14,045
- So, how much further?

1309
00:50:14,112 --> 00:50:16,347
- Taylor, help me!

1310
00:50:16,415 --> 00:50:18,250
Taylor, for God’s sake,
you can’t!

1311
00:50:18,317 --> 00:50:19,650
Help me!

1312
00:50:19,718 --> 00:50:22,187
- I haven’t the guts.
Remember, Jonathan?

1313
00:50:22,253 --> 00:50:24,488
- Oh, throw that in my face!

1314
00:50:25,690 --> 00:50:27,292
- Help!

1315
00:50:27,358 --> 00:50:28,593
( Jonathan screaming )

1316
00:50:28,660 --> 00:50:31,162
- How am I gonna explain this
in just barks?

1317
00:50:33,098 --> 00:50:35,700
- Now, lunch.

1318
00:50:35,767 --> 00:50:38,002
Oh, poopy.
Where did I put that rock?

1319
00:50:38,070 --> 00:50:39,170
Oh, boy.

1320
00:50:39,237 --> 00:50:42,707
( Suspenseful music )

1321
00:50:46,645 --> 00:50:48,413
- Oh, oh, hi, you.

1322
00:50:48,480 --> 00:50:51,215
I bet you’re wondering
what I’m doing up here.

1323
00:50:51,282 --> 00:50:52,517
TAYLOR:
Hello, Shep.

1324
00:50:52,583 --> 00:50:54,185
- This is where Shep’s
mastery of the stiletto

1325
00:50:54,252 --> 00:50:55,553
comes in handy.

1326
00:50:57,422 --> 00:51:00,292
- Look, I don’t even
want to talk to you.

1327
00:51:00,358 --> 00:51:02,327
- Here, Shep, come here!

1328
00:51:02,393 --> 00:51:04,997
- A lifetime of Snausages,
or I sing like a canary.

1329
00:51:07,632 --> 00:51:09,533
- What’s the matter with you?

1330
00:51:09,600 --> 00:51:12,270
- Uh, Mr. Weatherwax,
what’s the matter with me here?

1331
00:51:12,337 --> 00:51:14,305
I don’t know. Do you?

1332
00:51:14,372 --> 00:51:18,443
My trainer says
I’m not allowed to talk to you.

1333
00:51:18,510 --> 00:51:20,445
- I don’t think his testimony
will hold up in court.

1334
00:51:20,512 --> 00:51:21,713
- Mm-hm.

1335
00:51:27,385 --> 00:51:29,087
- Oh, he’ll probably be
in a wheelchair,

1336
00:51:29,153 --> 00:51:31,022
and I’m gonna have to
push him around.

1337
00:51:33,090 --> 00:51:37,162
Oh, geez, he looks like
Pile On Pete’s stew.

1338
00:51:39,397 --> 00:51:40,565
- It’s like Batman in reverse.

1339
00:51:40,632 --> 00:51:42,400
- Mm-hm.

1340
00:51:42,467 --> 00:51:43,668
- Thank you, citizen.

1341
00:51:45,503 --> 00:51:47,438
- Great, first I’ve gotta dig
him out of the dirt,

1342
00:51:47,505 --> 00:51:49,607
and he slides down
a dang mountain.

1343
00:51:49,673 --> 00:51:50,608
Aw, forget it.

1344
00:51:50,675 --> 00:51:51,977
Time to get a new master.

1345
00:51:53,645 --> 00:51:54,645
- Cinnamons.

1346
00:51:57,015 --> 00:52:01,452
- Like I always say, a dog’s
got to know her limitations.

1347
00:52:01,520 --> 00:52:04,788
- Ah, now, lunch.

1348
00:52:09,593 --> 00:52:12,363
- Tyrone Power
has a sleepless night.

1349
00:52:12,430 --> 00:52:17,068
- No, no, no, Pile On,
no, please, no.

1350
00:52:19,237 --> 00:52:21,740
- It’s payback time.

1351
00:52:24,008 --> 00:52:25,577
( Shouting/applause )

1352
00:52:25,643 --> 00:52:26,678
- Cool.

1353
00:52:26,745 --> 00:52:27,812
( Sniffing )

1354
00:52:27,878 --> 00:52:29,413
- Is that a bacon
and Snausage omelet?

1355
00:52:29,480 --> 00:52:31,315
What the?

1356
00:52:31,382 --> 00:52:32,950
( Shep sniffing )

1357
00:52:33,017 --> 00:52:35,087
- That’s where Jonathan’s butt
used to be.

1358
00:52:44,062 --> 00:52:45,430
- Hello, Shep.

1359
00:52:45,497 --> 00:52:49,333
- You can’t talk to me.
I saw what you did.

1360
00:52:52,037 --> 00:52:53,872
- Where have you been all day?

1361
00:52:53,938 --> 00:52:55,373
Looking for Jonathan?

1362
00:52:55,440 --> 00:52:57,875
- No, I caught a matinee
of "Oh, Heavenly Dog."

1363
00:52:57,942 --> 00:52:59,843
- I’m afraid
he won’t be back, Shep.

1364
00:52:59,912 --> 00:53:02,747
That doesn’t mean
you and I can’t be friends.

1365
00:53:02,813 --> 00:53:04,315
How about it?

1366
00:53:04,382 --> 00:53:07,085
- Look, just let me work
through my grief right now.

1367
00:53:07,152 --> 00:53:08,353
- Better be my way, Shep.

1368
00:53:08,420 --> 00:53:09,820
- Or the highway.

1369
00:53:09,888 --> 00:53:11,322
- I’d rather not have
anything happen to you.

1370
00:53:11,388 --> 00:53:13,825
Might make things
harder for me to explain.

1371
00:53:13,892 --> 00:53:15,627
- No, no, no,
don’t talk to me!

1372
00:53:15,693 --> 00:53:17,395
Stay on your side
of the room!

1373
00:53:20,832 --> 00:53:22,800
- Oh, look, if you don’t--
I’m talking to a collie.

1374
00:53:22,867 --> 00:53:24,335
Geez, I am freaking out.

1375
00:53:28,473 --> 00:53:30,408
- Uh, let’s see,
my cash card, my keys.

1376
00:53:30,475 --> 00:53:33,077
Lassie, you,
you seen my wallet?

1377
00:53:34,312 --> 00:53:35,413
- Oh, that’ll help,

1378
00:53:35,480 --> 00:53:37,382
since there’s no glass
in the window!

1379
00:53:39,383 --> 00:53:40,585
- I’m walking out this door,
Lassie,

1380
00:53:40,652 --> 00:53:42,020
and I’m never coming back.

1381
00:53:44,957 --> 00:53:48,560
- I hate my roommate so bad.

1382
00:53:48,627 --> 00:53:50,828
( Wolf howling )

1383
00:53:50,895 --> 00:53:53,197
- I hear you, man.

1384
00:53:53,265 --> 00:53:54,498
- Othello.

1385
00:53:54,565 --> 00:53:56,500
( Wolf howling )

1386
00:53:56,567 --> 00:53:58,970
- I left the iron on!

1387
00:53:59,037 --> 00:54:00,438
- Wait a minute!

1388
00:54:00,505 --> 00:54:02,440
I’m in "Seven Brides
for Seven Brothers."

1389
00:54:04,908 --> 00:54:06,310
Oh, Shep.

1390
00:54:06,377 --> 00:54:08,980
Oh, I’ll have to
clean that up later.

1391
00:54:12,550 --> 00:54:15,620
( Bot humming )

1392
00:54:15,687 --> 00:54:18,423
- Oh, I don’t want to see this.

1393
00:54:18,490 --> 00:54:20,158
- Get away from there!
Get away, I tell you!

1394
00:54:20,225 --> 00:54:21,158
- No!

1395
00:54:23,895 --> 00:54:25,397
- Hey, I’m a dog.
Come on.

1396
00:54:25,463 --> 00:54:27,032
- I said get away from there.

1397
00:54:27,098 --> 00:54:28,065
( Shep growling )

1398
00:54:28,132 --> 00:54:29,968
- I’m saying--
( BOT GROWLING )

1399
00:54:30,035 --> 00:54:31,268
- Get away,
or you’ll get what he got.

1400
00:54:31,335 --> 00:54:35,240
- Rave on, sister.
All right, woman.

1401
00:54:35,307 --> 00:54:36,942
- Okay, okay,
you can have the body,

1402
00:54:37,008 --> 00:54:38,743
but I get the gold.

1403
00:54:38,810 --> 00:54:41,145
( Shep growling )

1404
00:54:41,212 --> 00:54:42,347
- I don’t feel like fetching!

1405
00:54:43,848 --> 00:54:46,050
- Looks like he buried the old
guy and his hat separately.

1406
00:54:46,117 --> 00:54:50,022
( Shep growling )

1407
00:54:50,088 --> 00:54:52,790
- Lambada,
the forbidden dance.

1408
00:54:52,857 --> 00:54:55,760
( Lin screaming )

1409
00:54:55,827 --> 00:54:56,862
- Snausages.

1410
00:54:58,697 --> 00:55:00,898
- Seven o’clock already?

1411
00:55:03,100 --> 00:55:06,470
- Great, now a series of
very painful rabies shots.

1412
00:55:09,773 --> 00:55:14,145
- Oh, Lassie,
come out and play!

1413
00:55:14,212 --> 00:55:17,115
- I love to watch him squirm.

1414
00:55:19,417 --> 00:55:22,053
- Dope, you walked
right past me.

1415
00:55:22,120 --> 00:55:24,722
- I miss Lassie.

1416
00:55:24,788 --> 00:55:26,457
( Shep whining )

1417
00:55:26,523 --> 00:55:30,962
Ah, I’ve gotta get that dog
her own key.

1418
00:55:32,197 --> 00:55:36,233
- Hello?
Hi, I’m from PETA.

1419
00:55:37,968 --> 00:55:41,138
Ah, you’re probably mad.
Look, I was drunk last night.

1420
00:55:41,205 --> 00:55:43,575
I’m sorry.

1421
00:55:43,642 --> 00:55:44,575
- Hungry, huh?

1422
00:55:44,642 --> 00:55:45,943
( Shep whining )

1423
00:55:46,010 --> 00:55:47,878
I figured that would
bring you around,

1424
00:55:47,945 --> 00:55:50,182
and I’ve got something
all ready for you.

1425
00:55:53,552 --> 00:55:54,585
- Gonna be a Snausage, right?

1426
00:55:54,652 --> 00:55:57,022
Oh, please,
let it be a Snausage.

1427
00:55:58,823 --> 00:56:00,625
- Oh, I put up
a lot of poison last year,

1428
00:56:00,692 --> 00:56:04,195
but now we’re just down
to the one jar.

1429
00:56:04,262 --> 00:56:05,897
- It’s a deadly game
of cat and mouse,

1430
00:56:05,963 --> 00:56:08,132
only with a dog and a man.

1431
00:56:08,198 --> 00:56:10,435
- You know, the 1800’s
were a golden age of gravy.

1432
00:56:10,502 --> 00:56:11,870
- Aw.

1433
00:56:11,937 --> 00:56:13,872
- Here, I hope you like
your Fritos.

1434
00:56:16,340 --> 00:56:20,712
- Oh, no, I had poison
last night.

1435
00:56:20,778 --> 00:56:23,482
- Hey, that’s good poison.

1436
00:56:23,548 --> 00:56:26,083
- Not hungry enough yet, huh?

1437
00:56:26,150 --> 00:56:27,752
You will be.

1438
00:56:27,818 --> 00:56:31,122
- I believe I ordered
a Greek salad.

1439
00:56:31,188 --> 00:56:35,327
Uh, can I just have
a rye crisp, please?

1440
00:56:35,393 --> 00:56:37,395
- Little does he know
Lassie switched food.

1441
00:56:37,462 --> 00:56:39,563
He’s eating Kal Kan!

1442
00:56:39,630 --> 00:56:40,965
( Shep whining )

1443
00:56:41,032 --> 00:56:42,167
- You know, Lassie’s
not really that hungry.

1444
00:56:42,233 --> 00:56:44,235
She just dug up Jonathan
and, well, uh--

1445
00:56:44,302 --> 00:56:46,470
- Oh.
- Hm.

1446
00:56:46,537 --> 00:56:48,607
- That’s an odd taste.

1447
00:56:48,673 --> 00:56:52,243
Oh, I can’t stay mad at you.

1448
00:56:52,310 --> 00:56:53,878
- Is this what you want?

1449
00:56:53,945 --> 00:56:56,213
( Shep barking )

1450
00:56:59,317 --> 00:57:00,818
- So that’s the way
it is, huh?

1451
00:57:00,885 --> 00:57:02,587
- Mm-hm, mm-hm.

1452
00:57:02,653 --> 00:57:03,888
- Here.

1453
00:57:03,955 --> 00:57:05,890
( Shep barking )

1454
00:57:11,662 --> 00:57:13,732
- Mm-mm. Souvlaki?

1455
00:57:13,798 --> 00:57:15,098
( BOT LAUGHING SINISTERLY )
- More?

1456
00:57:15,167 --> 00:57:16,567
( Shep barking )

1457
00:57:16,633 --> 00:57:20,805
- Puppy Chow for a full year
’til he’s full dead.

1458
00:57:20,872 --> 00:57:23,608
- Oh, no, get the Ipecac.

1459
00:57:23,675 --> 00:57:24,875
- Eat it.

1460
00:57:24,942 --> 00:57:26,210
- Now get out of here.
Get out!

1461
00:57:26,277 --> 00:57:27,412
And remember,
you asked for it.

1462
00:57:27,478 --> 00:57:28,813
- Go die.
Die outside now.

1463
00:57:28,880 --> 00:57:30,448
Come on.

1464
00:57:30,515 --> 00:57:31,715
- Yeah, I thought so.

1465
00:57:31,782 --> 00:57:34,085
I get better service
at Planet Hollywood.

1466
00:57:36,187 --> 00:57:37,388
- He’s not gonna die, is he?

1467
00:57:37,455 --> 00:57:38,923
- Oh, yeah, she’s toast.

1468
00:57:38,990 --> 00:57:42,227
Old Lassie dead, dead, dead,
deader than a door nail, yep.

1469
00:57:44,562 --> 00:57:46,898
- Oh, this is where Lassie
gets the inspiration

1470
00:57:46,965 --> 00:57:48,533
for her poetry.

1471
00:57:48,600 --> 00:57:50,168
- Dude, she’s gonna die.

1472
00:57:50,235 --> 00:57:52,903
- Boy, do I feel stupid.
It’s more my pride that hurts.

1473
00:57:52,970 --> 00:57:54,905
( Shep whining )

1474
00:57:54,972 --> 00:57:56,707
- Come on,
it’s gut chuck time, Lassie.

1475
00:57:56,773 --> 00:57:59,110
Find it, find it!

1476
00:57:59,177 --> 00:58:00,578
Oh, there she is,
200 yards.

1477
00:58:00,645 --> 00:58:01,980
You could make it
with iron sights,

1478
00:58:02,047 --> 00:58:04,315
but you want to
use a scope, Crow.

1479
00:58:04,382 --> 00:58:05,650
- Hey.

1480
00:58:07,752 --> 00:58:10,422
- Geez, a river runs through it,
and grapefruit runs through me,

1481
00:58:10,488 --> 00:58:11,788
I tell you, oh.

1482
00:58:11,857 --> 00:58:15,260
- I can’t believe
I ate the whole thing.

1483
00:58:15,327 --> 00:58:17,695
- If I could just burp,
I’d feel better.

1484
00:58:17,762 --> 00:58:18,697
( Shep whining )

1485
00:58:18,763 --> 00:58:19,797
- Oh, she’s a goner.

1486
00:58:19,863 --> 00:58:21,398
Probably having hallucinations,
right now,

1487
00:58:21,465 --> 00:58:23,868
a little wagon coming out
of the kitchen cabinet.

1488
00:58:23,935 --> 00:58:24,868
( Chuckling )

1489
00:58:24,935 --> 00:58:25,970
Yep, two paws in the grave.

1490
00:58:26,037 --> 00:58:27,072
- Hey, stop it!

1491
00:58:27,138 --> 00:58:28,573
- I’m just being a realist,
Crow.

1492
00:58:31,442 --> 00:58:36,080
- This exercise will give
your dog buns of steel.

1493
00:58:36,147 --> 00:58:37,782
- What is this?
Camille?

1494
00:58:37,848 --> 00:58:38,783
- I don’t know.

1495
00:58:40,083 --> 00:58:41,385
- Crawl!
Come on,

1496
00:58:41,452 --> 00:58:43,153
you’ve never given up
on anything in your life!

1497
00:58:43,220 --> 00:58:45,523
Crawl!

1498
00:58:45,590 --> 00:58:51,528
( Making silly sounds like
curly of the three stooges )

1499
00:58:51,595 --> 00:58:55,132
( Shep whining/barking )

1500
00:58:55,198 --> 00:58:58,803
- I’m coming, Elizabeth.
I’m coming to join you.

1501
00:58:58,870 --> 00:59:02,973
- I don’t drink near the horses,
personally.

1502
00:59:03,040 --> 00:59:04,675
- You know,
that little puppy’s dead, too.

1503
00:59:04,742 --> 00:59:07,145
- Oh!
- Sorry.

1504
00:59:09,280 --> 00:59:10,348
( Shep whining )

1505
00:59:10,415 --> 00:59:12,617
- Boy, is she milking
this thing.

1506
00:59:12,683 --> 00:59:14,352
- Oh, don’t you
chew the scenery.

1507
00:59:14,418 --> 00:59:16,520
Don’t you--
drop it, drop it.

1508
00:59:16,587 --> 00:59:18,623
- Hey, I’m dying over here!

1509
00:59:20,157 --> 00:59:21,392
- Lassie,
does that really help?

1510
00:59:21,458 --> 00:59:22,760
- When she’s dying,

1511
00:59:22,827 --> 00:59:24,728
she’s going to take as many ants
with her as possible.

1512
00:59:28,633 --> 00:59:29,833
- Would you keep it down?

1513
00:59:34,505 --> 00:59:36,007
Oh, the great Lassie
is in trouble.

1514
00:59:36,073 --> 00:59:37,942
Well, I’ve stood in your shadow
long enough!

1515
00:59:41,778 --> 00:59:43,682
Lassie’s been drinking again.

1516
00:59:46,583 --> 00:59:48,787
- You know, they’re probably
dead, too, in fact.

1517
00:59:48,853 --> 00:59:49,853
- Tom, that’s enough.

1518
00:59:49,920 --> 00:59:51,555
- Sorry.

1519
00:59:51,622 --> 00:59:53,457
- He’s helping.
He’s pumping her stomach.

1520
00:59:55,793 --> 00:59:58,162
- I need bicarb
and a hot needle here, stat!

1521
00:59:58,228 --> 00:59:59,863
Come on!

1522
00:59:59,930 --> 01:00:02,867
( Small dog barking )

1523
01:00:04,268 --> 01:00:06,870
- Well, now he’s massaging
her heart, oh.

1524
01:00:06,937 --> 01:00:09,873
( Small dog barking )

1525
01:00:13,043 --> 01:00:15,212
- ( Speaking in
native language )

1526
01:00:15,278 --> 01:00:16,780
- Yeah, that’s Indian,
all right.

1527
01:00:16,847 --> 01:00:18,548
- Gibberish.
- I can hear the ocean roar.

1528
01:00:18,615 --> 01:00:21,685
- ( Speaking in
native language )

1529
01:00:21,752 --> 01:00:23,655
- His tag says he’s allergic
to penicillin.

1530
01:00:25,288 --> 01:00:26,590
- Hold on.

1531
01:00:31,328 --> 01:00:32,663
- Oh, yeah, nice heft.

1532
01:00:32,730 --> 01:00:35,032
This hide should bring in
ten bucks in town.

1533
01:00:37,735 --> 01:00:39,170
- Natalie Wood.
- Now, these are scenes

1534
01:00:39,237 --> 01:00:40,705
we didn’t see in
"The Searchers."

1535
01:00:43,307 --> 01:00:44,808
- Bald Eagle in town.
Find quick.

1536
01:00:44,875 --> 01:00:46,143
Maybe he save dog.

1537
01:00:46,210 --> 01:00:47,778
- It’s a tribal
Pippi Longstockings.

1538
01:00:47,845 --> 01:00:51,048
( Shouting )

1539
01:00:51,115 --> 01:00:54,185
( Bot whistling like siren )

1540
01:00:55,787 --> 01:00:57,155
- ♪ Sobbin’ women ♪

1541
01:00:57,222 --> 01:00:59,357
♪ Them sobbin’,
sobbin’ women ♪

1542
01:00:59,423 --> 01:01:02,260
( Shouting )

1543
01:01:02,327 --> 01:01:04,028
- Huh?

1544
01:01:04,095 --> 01:01:07,898
- All his woodland friends
are coming to help him.

1545
01:01:07,965 --> 01:01:11,902
( Bot humming excitedly )

1546
01:01:11,968 --> 01:01:13,738
- ( Speaking in
native language )

1547
01:01:13,805 --> 01:01:15,607
- Hi!
Hey, what’s going on?

1548
01:01:15,673 --> 01:01:17,075
- Oh, I saw them march down
at the post office.

1549
01:01:17,142 --> 01:01:18,442
- Oh.

1550
01:01:18,508 --> 01:01:21,212
- ( Speaking in
native language )

1551
01:01:23,213 --> 01:01:24,948
- Is that Russell Means?
Ha, ha.

1552
01:01:27,952 --> 01:01:30,120
- Good heavens, boy,
you’re right.

1553
01:01:31,555 --> 01:01:33,758
Oh, no, poison souvlaki.

1554
01:01:36,660 --> 01:01:38,897
- ( Speaking in
native language )

1555
01:01:46,603 --> 01:01:49,040
- Yeah, go get Jeff Chandler
and Joey Bishop.

1556
01:01:50,307 --> 01:01:52,877
- Who am I gonna bill for this?
Wait a minute!

1557
01:01:52,943 --> 01:01:55,880
( Theme music playing )

1558
01:02:00,418 --> 01:02:02,320
- This’ll help,
a feather in a dilly bar.

1559
01:02:02,387 --> 01:02:04,288
- ( Speaking in
native language )

1560
01:02:04,355 --> 01:02:08,025
- Oh, wait,
I was doing a bar mitzvah.

1561
01:02:08,092 --> 01:02:10,995
- Sorry, kid,
doesn’t sound good.

1562
01:02:13,497 --> 01:02:15,032
- Well, she will make
an attractive rug.

1563
01:02:15,098 --> 01:02:16,567
- Hey!
- Sorry.

1564
01:02:18,468 --> 01:02:20,203
- Please, Mr. Bald Eagle.

1565
01:02:20,270 --> 01:02:21,805
Isn’t there anything else
you can do?

1566
01:02:21,872 --> 01:02:23,273
- Well, I can do impressions.

1567
01:02:26,210 --> 01:02:28,980
( Making silly sounds like
curly of the three stooges )

1568
01:02:29,047 --> 01:02:30,982
- Well, this is
cross-cultural, isn’t it?

1569
01:02:31,048 --> 01:02:32,117
- Uh-huh.

1570
01:02:34,218 --> 01:02:36,153
- Are those Snausages
over there?

1571
01:02:38,022 --> 01:02:39,190
( Shep whining )

1572
01:02:39,257 --> 01:02:41,758
- Shep, Shep, girl.

1573
01:02:41,825 --> 01:02:44,262
Mr. Bald Eagle,
you’re wonderful.

1574
01:02:44,328 --> 01:02:45,563
Thanks--

1575
01:02:45,630 --> 01:02:49,700
( speaking native language )

1576
01:02:49,767 --> 01:02:50,702
- That’s outrageous!

1577
01:02:50,768 --> 01:02:52,537
I want to see
an itemized bill!

1578
01:02:52,603 --> 01:02:53,872
- ( Speaking native language )

1579
01:02:53,938 --> 01:02:56,140
- Wow, his teepee’s
really nice.

1580
01:02:58,208 --> 01:02:59,277
- I can’t tell you
how stupid I feel

1581
01:02:59,343 --> 01:03:01,645
about the whole poison thing,
Tommy.

1582
01:03:01,712 --> 01:03:02,947
- Don’t you worry, Shep.

1583
01:03:03,013 --> 01:03:04,315
- Okay.

1584
01:03:04,382 --> 01:03:06,250
- I’m gonna find out
about that poison.

1585
01:03:06,317 --> 01:03:09,587
Nothing’s gonna stop me
’til I do.

1586
01:03:09,653 --> 01:03:11,455
- This is God. Stop!

1587
01:03:11,522 --> 01:03:14,458
( Rumbling thunder )

1588
01:03:19,463 --> 01:03:22,500
- Oh, I just can’t get
this darn novel started.

1589
01:03:22,567 --> 01:03:24,202
- It’ll be really bad
while the weather holds.

1590
01:03:24,268 --> 01:03:26,870
- Hey, it sounds like
a Ken Nordine dream.

1591
01:03:26,937 --> 01:03:29,873
- One good stone
could start a slide

1592
01:03:29,940 --> 01:03:31,842
that would cover the gold
forever.

1593
01:03:31,908 --> 01:03:33,443
- The old man never said that.

1594
01:03:33,510 --> 01:03:35,045
- You can’t flash back to
something that never happened.

1595
01:03:35,112 --> 01:03:36,180
It’s not fair.

1596
01:03:36,247 --> 01:03:39,517
( Rumble of rocks sliding )

1597
01:03:41,818 --> 01:03:43,753
- Now the car’s rolling.

1598
01:03:43,820 --> 01:03:45,288
( Rumble of rocks sliding )

1599
01:03:47,592 --> 01:03:49,893
Is that me?
Am I making that sound?

1600
01:03:54,365 --> 01:03:55,867
( Rumbling thunder )

1601
01:03:55,933 --> 01:03:58,302
Boy, Dutch Elm
can strike quick.

1602
01:03:58,368 --> 01:04:00,805
- Hmph, dog gone,
don’t see that every day.

1603
01:04:00,872 --> 01:04:02,307
Well, back to the book.

1604
01:04:05,175 --> 01:04:06,543
- Vandalism.

1605
01:04:06,610 --> 01:04:09,580
Dang kids drink cheap wine,
listen to Anthrax.

1606
01:04:09,647 --> 01:04:11,415
Look at this.
Ah, hm.

1607
01:04:13,150 --> 01:04:14,085
Well, thank you, God.

1608
01:04:14,152 --> 01:04:16,420
Thank you so bloody much!

1609
01:04:17,522 --> 01:04:19,490
Hey, divinity.

1610
01:04:21,825 --> 01:04:26,963
( Bot humming theme song from
"2001: A space odyssey" )

1611
01:04:27,030 --> 01:04:29,533
( Suspenseful music )

1612
01:04:34,305 --> 01:04:35,573
- Oh, so that’s the lesson,
Joel?

1613
01:04:35,640 --> 01:04:36,873
Crime does pay?

1614
01:04:36,940 --> 01:04:38,375
- No, that’s not the lesson.

1615
01:04:38,442 --> 01:04:40,310
- No, the damage is done.
It’s in my memory banks.

1616
01:04:40,377 --> 01:04:42,112
Crime pays-- zip, lock.

1617
01:04:45,015 --> 01:04:46,250
- Look at that dog gone ridge.

1618
01:04:46,317 --> 01:04:47,818
- Hey.

1619
01:04:47,885 --> 01:04:49,787
- Shep’s back, and this time
it’s personal.

1620
01:04:49,853 --> 01:04:51,388
TOMMY:
Shep, come here!

1621
01:04:51,455 --> 01:04:52,823
- Shep--
TOMMY: Where are you going?

1622
01:04:52,890 --> 01:04:56,293
- You’ve got to write to
Mr. Eagle, a thank you note.

1623
01:04:56,360 --> 01:04:57,595
- The old guy is still alive,

1624
01:04:57,662 --> 01:04:59,797
’cause he had a straw with him
to breathe with.

1625
01:04:59,863 --> 01:05:01,632
- Or he’s a hellish zombie.

1626
01:05:01,698 --> 01:05:04,835
( Suspenseful music )

1627
01:05:04,902 --> 01:05:05,837
- Wait a minute.

1628
01:05:05,903 --> 01:05:07,638
These bags
are full of Snausages.

1629
01:05:07,705 --> 01:05:08,940
Lassie!

1630
01:05:09,007 --> 01:05:10,240
- Oh.

1631
01:05:11,808 --> 01:05:15,112
- It’s easier than balancing
your checkbook, believe me.

1632
01:05:15,178 --> 01:05:16,113
( Wolf howling )

1633
01:05:16,180 --> 01:05:18,248
- Oh, I’m a bad boy!

1634
01:05:20,685 --> 01:05:23,587
- Taylor is almost
wolfman-ish.

1635
01:05:23,653 --> 01:05:26,423
- Yeah, he gave himself
a Lilt home perm.

1636
01:05:29,927 --> 01:05:31,895
- Stay off the moors!

1637
01:05:36,800 --> 01:05:37,935
- Shep!

1638
01:05:38,002 --> 01:05:40,170
- Don’t kid yourself!

1639
01:05:47,678 --> 01:05:50,613
( Bots whistling )

1640
01:05:53,317 --> 01:05:55,252
- I’m looking for a man
with true grit.

1641
01:05:58,855 --> 01:06:00,090
- The bottom of the fifth,

1642
01:06:00,157 --> 01:06:02,192
and Lassie paces the mound
nervously.

1643
01:06:04,362 --> 01:06:05,797
( Whistling theme from
"lassie" tv show )

1644
01:06:05,863 --> 01:06:07,732
( Shep barking )

1645
01:06:07,798 --> 01:06:08,732
- Yeah, that’s it.

1646
01:06:08,798 --> 01:06:10,333
- This dog is dumb as a post.

1647
01:06:10,400 --> 01:06:13,337
( Shep barking )

1648
01:06:14,472 --> 01:06:17,307
- Here, puppy.
Come here, puppy.

1649
01:06:17,375 --> 01:06:20,043
Yes, puppy.
- What the?

1650
01:06:20,110 --> 01:06:22,480
Hey, if he shoots the kid,
all bets are off.

1651
01:06:22,547 --> 01:06:23,813
We’re gonna
trash the theater!

1652
01:06:23,880 --> 01:06:24,948
- Yep.

1653
01:06:25,015 --> 01:06:27,718
- I’m bringing home the baby--
oh.

1654
01:06:27,785 --> 01:06:28,918
- Jonathan.

1655
01:06:28,985 --> 01:06:30,120
- Tommy, what are you
doing here?

1656
01:06:30,187 --> 01:06:33,023
- What a mess!
What have you been doing here?

1657
01:06:33,090 --> 01:06:34,925
- Where’s Jonathan?

1658
01:06:34,992 --> 01:06:37,227
- He’s gone.
He’s been gone for three days.

1659
01:06:37,293 --> 01:06:39,263
- Where?
- I don’t know.

1660
01:06:39,330 --> 01:06:40,497
Over towards Blue Mountain
somewhere.

1661
01:06:40,563 --> 01:06:41,998
- Over to the mega mall.

1662
01:06:42,065 --> 01:06:43,600
- He went to pick us up a deer
for meat through the winter.

1663
01:06:43,667 --> 01:06:45,202
- Camp Snoopy.

1664
01:06:45,268 --> 01:06:47,337
- I heard his whistle.
- You couldn’t have.

1665
01:06:47,405 --> 01:06:50,140
- Shep must’ve, too.
She was barking.

1666
01:06:50,207 --> 01:06:53,110
- Where’d you see, Shep?
- Indian kids found her.

1667
01:06:53,177 --> 01:06:55,112
She was almost dead.
- I didn’t do it.

1668
01:06:55,178 --> 01:06:57,882
- From poison.
- But she’s all right now?

1669
01:06:57,948 --> 01:07:00,083
- Yeah, but I knew Jonathan
would want to know,

1670
01:07:00,150 --> 01:07:01,185
so I brought her back.

1671
01:07:01,252 --> 01:07:02,587
- Dang. I mean, good.

1672
01:07:02,653 --> 01:07:04,155
- Mr. Taylor, are you sure
he’s not here?

1673
01:07:04,222 --> 01:07:05,522
I know I heard.

1674
01:07:05,588 --> 01:07:08,125
- I told you, Tommy,
he’s not here.

1675
01:07:08,192 --> 01:07:10,560
- Please, sir,
may I have some more?

1676
01:07:10,627 --> 01:07:13,530
- Shep! Shep!

1677
01:07:13,597 --> 01:07:15,933
- Psst, hey, stupid kid,
I’m up here.

1678
01:07:16,000 --> 01:07:17,033
- Here, Shep!

1679
01:07:19,837 --> 01:07:23,007
Why won’t she come in?
I know she’s out there.

1680
01:07:23,073 --> 01:07:24,642
- Let me tell you about
the after life, Tommy.

1681
01:07:24,708 --> 01:07:27,412
- Don’t worry about her, Tommy.
You’re tired and upset.

1682
01:07:27,478 --> 01:07:28,745
A good night’s sleep,

1683
01:07:28,812 --> 01:07:30,113
and you can start home
in the morning.

1684
01:07:30,180 --> 01:07:32,148
Sit down now and rest.

1685
01:07:32,215 --> 01:07:34,685
- Tommy, you like refried
poison-- beans, beans!?

1686
01:07:36,820 --> 01:07:39,022
Oh, boy.

1687
01:07:39,090 --> 01:07:41,292
- I’ll fix you some supper.

1688
01:07:41,358 --> 01:07:44,362
( Sinister laughing )

1689
01:07:46,897 --> 01:07:48,198
- Well, cling peaches.

1690
01:07:48,265 --> 01:07:50,567
Button popped up,
but that’s okay.

1691
01:07:50,633 --> 01:07:52,437
- We’re having Beefaroni!

1692
01:07:52,503 --> 01:07:54,405
- Yeah, one gram of poison
per pound.

1693
01:07:54,472 --> 01:07:56,940
Hey, how much do you weigh, kid?

1694
01:07:57,007 --> 01:08:01,112
- I, I’d better unsaddle
my horse, Mr. Taylor.

1695
01:08:01,178 --> 01:08:03,047
I’ll be right back.

1696
01:08:03,113 --> 01:08:05,817
- I’d better think of a flimsy
excuse to leave, Mr. Taylor.

1697
01:08:08,952 --> 01:08:11,455
( Shep whining )

1698
01:08:11,522 --> 01:08:14,658
- Shep, what is it?
- It’s a grave.

1699
01:08:14,725 --> 01:08:16,327
- What is it, Shep?
- It’s a grave!

1700
01:08:16,393 --> 01:08:17,728
Do I have to draw you
a picture?

1701
01:08:17,795 --> 01:08:20,130
Look, here’s you, me,
this is the grave. Come on.

1702
01:08:20,197 --> 01:08:22,098
- Eww, gross!

1703
01:08:22,165 --> 01:08:23,133
- Jonathan.

1704
01:08:23,200 --> 01:08:25,068
( Shep whining )

1705
01:08:25,135 --> 01:08:27,037
- Shep, you killed Jonathan!

1706
01:08:28,572 --> 01:08:30,707
- Life is pain, Tommy.

1707
01:08:35,145 --> 01:08:36,613
- Yeah, well, anyway,
there’s more evidence over here.

1708
01:08:36,680 --> 01:08:38,415
Come on.
Come on!

1709
01:08:38,482 --> 01:08:40,383
( Shep barking )

1710
01:08:40,450 --> 01:08:41,685
- Oh, my God,
another grave!

1711
01:08:41,752 --> 01:08:43,753
Lassie’s got him
buried everywhere!

1712
01:08:43,820 --> 01:08:44,755
( Shep barking )

1713
01:08:44,822 --> 01:08:46,323
- Shep, be quiet.

1714
01:08:46,390 --> 01:08:48,392
- Come on, your poison’s
getting cold.

1715
01:08:48,458 --> 01:08:50,793
- Shh.

1716
01:08:50,862 --> 01:08:52,095
- Hm?

1717
01:08:52,162 --> 01:08:54,130
- Oh, Jonathan’s
dangerously skinny.

1718
01:08:57,133 --> 01:08:58,168
- Watch the prints, Tommy.

1719
01:08:58,235 --> 01:08:59,603
Heck, Ramsey needs that hat.

1720
01:08:59,670 --> 01:09:02,038
- Yes, it’s Jonathan’s.

1721
01:09:02,105 --> 01:09:03,740
- It has his stench.

1722
01:09:05,642 --> 01:09:06,577
TAYLOR:
Tommy?

1723
01:09:06,643 --> 01:09:08,212
- Can you hear me?

1724
01:09:08,278 --> 01:09:09,213
TAYLOR:
Tommy!

1725
01:09:09,280 --> 01:09:10,480
- Can you hear me?

1726
01:09:10,547 --> 01:09:12,783
( Suspenseful music )

1727
01:09:16,253 --> 01:09:18,555
- Tommy!
Tommy, you out here?

1728
01:09:18,622 --> 01:09:20,157
- He’s being followed
by a doughboy.

1729
01:09:20,223 --> 01:09:21,158
( Chuckling )

1730
01:09:23,627 --> 01:09:26,197
- Danged smoochers
on my property!

1731
01:09:26,263 --> 01:09:28,132
Come out, come out
wherever you are!

1732
01:09:28,198 --> 01:09:30,500
- Tommy!
Tommy, is that you?

1733
01:09:30,567 --> 01:09:33,470
- Tonight, Tommy goes
behind enemy lines.

1734
01:09:33,537 --> 01:09:35,472
( Humming them from
"the great escape" )

1735
01:09:45,950 --> 01:09:47,283
- Joel!

1736
01:09:47,350 --> 01:09:50,853
Servo went and melted me
into a golden god!

1737
01:09:50,922 --> 01:09:52,022
And it itches
a little bit.

1738
01:09:52,088 --> 01:09:54,658
- Meets the new gold standard,
Joel, yes.

1739
01:09:54,725 --> 01:09:56,527
Our very own Crow.

1740
01:09:56,593 --> 01:09:58,828
( Sinister laughing )

1741
01:09:58,895 --> 01:10:03,100
- Tom, Crow is not gold.
He’s gold tone.

1742
01:10:03,167 --> 01:10:04,235
I painted him gold.

1743
01:10:04,302 --> 01:10:05,368
- Yeah.

1744
01:10:05,435 --> 01:10:06,570
- Oh, you’d like me
to believe that,

1745
01:10:06,637 --> 01:10:08,138
wouldn’t you, Mr. West?

1746
01:10:09,673 --> 01:10:13,110
- You know, Tom, Crow is
basically a high density Kevlar.

1747
01:10:13,177 --> 01:10:14,110
That’s it.

1748
01:10:14,177 --> 01:10:15,612
- Yeah. Really?
- Yeah.

1749
01:10:15,678 --> 01:10:18,348
- Trying to head me off
at the pass, hey, Joel?

1750
01:10:18,415 --> 01:10:19,717
- Tom, listen.

1751
01:10:19,783 --> 01:10:23,120
If you break Crow down
into his essential elements,

1752
01:10:23,187 --> 01:10:24,755
he’s virtually worthless.

1753
01:10:24,822 --> 01:10:26,090
- Yeah-- hey.

1754
01:10:26,157 --> 01:10:27,892
- How stupid do you think I am,
old man?

1755
01:10:27,958 --> 01:10:29,393
Put ’em up.

1756
01:10:29,460 --> 01:10:33,430
- Listen, up here, snack items
have more value than Crow does.

1757
01:10:33,497 --> 01:10:35,032
You’d be better off
staking your claim

1758
01:10:35,098 --> 01:10:37,067
to a box of Bearwiches,
Tom, okay?

1759
01:10:37,133 --> 01:10:40,503
- The point is, Joel,
he’s gold, and he’s mine.

1760
01:10:40,570 --> 01:10:41,638
( Sinister laughing )

1761
01:10:41,705 --> 01:10:43,840
Together,
I can rule the world!

1762
01:10:43,907 --> 01:10:46,243
( Sinister laughing )

1763
01:10:46,310 --> 01:10:48,112
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1764
01:10:48,178 --> 01:10:50,847
Hey, I’m really
worth a fortune.

1765
01:10:50,913 --> 01:10:52,515
In fact, I want me.

1766
01:10:52,582 --> 01:10:53,883
- Wait a minute.

1767
01:10:53,950 --> 01:10:56,087
- Yes, I’m mine!
I’m mine, all mine!

1768
01:10:56,153 --> 01:10:58,855
- Come on, dummy,
it’s time to extrude you.

1769
01:11:00,690 --> 01:11:03,627
( Theme music playing )

1770
01:11:07,998 --> 01:11:09,165
- Thank you for extruding me.

1771
01:11:09,232 --> 01:11:11,368
- Mm-hm.
- Sorry, Crow.

1772
01:11:14,705 --> 01:11:16,707
- No, no, let me go!
Let me go!

1773
01:11:16,773 --> 01:11:17,975
- Where’d you get it?
Where’d you get it?

1774
01:11:18,042 --> 01:11:19,910
- Craigie Way.
- It’s Jonathan’s!

1775
01:11:19,977 --> 01:11:21,045
You lied!
He isn’t away!

1776
01:11:21,112 --> 01:11:22,612
He’s dead!
You killed him!

1777
01:11:22,680 --> 01:11:24,715
He’s buried over there,
and you killed him!

1778
01:11:24,782 --> 01:11:26,283
- Shut up. Do you hear me?
Shut up!

1779
01:11:26,350 --> 01:11:28,252
- No, I won’t!
You killed him, and I’ll tell!

1780
01:11:28,318 --> 01:11:29,520
I’ll tell everybody!

1781
01:11:29,587 --> 01:11:31,722
- Oh, how could you
possibly tell everybody?

1782
01:11:31,788 --> 01:11:32,857
- You’re wrong, Tommy.

1783
01:11:32,923 --> 01:11:34,157
- You gonna tell the Chinese?

1784
01:11:34,223 --> 01:11:35,325
- You’re not gonna
tell anybody that.

1785
01:11:35,392 --> 01:11:36,660
- I’m gonna tell ’em.

1786
01:11:36,727 --> 01:11:38,228
- Shep, help!
- Shut up, you!

1787
01:11:38,295 --> 01:11:41,232
- Help, Shep!
- Ah, arf keba!

1788
01:11:41,298 --> 01:11:42,532
( Shep barking )

1789
01:11:42,598 --> 01:11:44,835
- Thanks, Shep.
I’m outta here. Good luck!

1790
01:11:44,902 --> 01:11:48,305
- Ah, Jim Collie!

1791
01:11:48,372 --> 01:11:50,607
( Shep barking )

1792
01:11:53,377 --> 01:11:55,412
- Dog tossing, tonight,
on "A Current Affair."

1793
01:11:55,478 --> 01:11:57,682
- Yeah, must’ve been
Lassie number 12 or so.

1794
01:11:59,817 --> 01:12:02,252
- When did they build a corral?
They used corral draw.

1795
01:12:02,318 --> 01:12:04,187
- Oh, you.

1796
01:12:04,253 --> 01:12:06,223
- Tommy!

1797
01:12:06,290 --> 01:12:07,290
- Come on, Apache, jump it!

1798
01:12:07,357 --> 01:12:09,493
- This must be
"Regional Velvet."

1799
01:12:09,560 --> 01:12:13,097
- Please, Apache,
you’ve gotta jump it.

1800
01:12:13,163 --> 01:12:15,365
You’ve gotta do it.
You’ve got to!

1801
01:12:15,432 --> 01:12:16,833
- Tommy, come back here!
Come back!

1802
01:12:16,900 --> 01:12:19,603
- Oh, you can’t expect him
to come back.

1803
01:12:19,670 --> 01:12:22,105
- Huh?

1804
01:12:22,172 --> 01:12:23,773
- Ah.

1805
01:12:23,840 --> 01:12:25,942
- So, when you’re the bad guy,
things just go your way.

1806
01:12:26,008 --> 01:12:27,077
Is that the lesson, Joel?

1807
01:12:27,143 --> 01:12:28,212
- No.

1808
01:12:28,278 --> 01:12:29,380
- You little fool,
I ought to--

1809
01:12:29,447 --> 01:12:30,613
- Oh, maybe I should see
if you’re alive.

1810
01:12:30,680 --> 01:12:32,415
Then I’ll kill you.

1811
01:12:32,482 --> 01:12:35,252
- Either this boy is dead,
or his heart has stopped.

1812
01:12:35,318 --> 01:12:37,120
( Suspenseful music )

1813
01:12:37,187 --> 01:12:39,423
- Kid, you’ve got
a French horn in your chest.

1814
01:12:41,292 --> 01:12:43,527
He can’t be dead.
He can’t be.

1815
01:12:43,593 --> 01:12:44,595
- Kaput, finis.

1816
01:12:44,662 --> 01:12:45,595
- Uh-uh.

1817
01:12:46,663 --> 01:12:48,965
- I love my dead, gay son.

1818
01:12:51,702 --> 01:12:54,137
- Uh, I, I, I, I, I, I--

1819
01:12:56,340 --> 01:12:57,842
( bots making sounds
like horse galloping )

1820
01:12:57,908 --> 01:13:01,278
- ♪ Now, when the west
was very young ♪

1821
01:13:01,345 --> 01:13:04,515
- Hey, look, he’s doing a shadow
thingy of Abe Lincoln--

1822
01:13:04,582 --> 01:13:06,083
Oh. Sorry.

1823
01:13:11,988 --> 01:13:13,923
- Now, wait, who is
that other shadow?

1824
01:13:13,990 --> 01:13:16,058
Is that Peter Pan
looking on?

1825
01:13:17,762 --> 01:13:19,563
- What a piece of work is man.

1826
01:13:25,235 --> 01:13:26,170
- Here, here’s some poison
for you.

1827
01:13:26,237 --> 01:13:28,605
- Now, come on, now, drink it.

1828
01:13:28,672 --> 01:13:29,673
- It’s Old Milwaukee.

1829
01:13:29,740 --> 01:13:31,942
- You’re all right now, here.

1830
01:13:32,008 --> 01:13:33,943
Drink, Tommy.
- Tastes as great as its name.

1831
01:13:34,010 --> 01:13:35,545
- Drink it.

1832
01:13:35,612 --> 01:13:36,780
That’s right.

1833
01:13:36,847 --> 01:13:38,282
- Taste it all--
one awesome calorie.

1834
01:13:38,348 --> 01:13:39,783
- That’s fine, boy.

1835
01:13:39,850 --> 01:13:41,818
- The first one is free, kid.
The next one’s on you.

1836
01:13:43,420 --> 01:13:47,992
- Yeah-- oh, and you were there,
and you, and you, and--

1837
01:13:48,058 --> 01:13:50,093
- Get away from me!
Leave me alone!

1838
01:13:50,160 --> 01:13:51,628
- Listen, son,
you’ve got to listen to me.

1839
01:13:51,695 --> 01:13:52,930
- I’m trying to redeem myself.

1840
01:13:52,997 --> 01:13:54,430
- There’s nothing
to be afraid of.

1841
01:13:54,498 --> 01:13:56,800
You’re here, perfectly safe,
and I brought you here.

1842
01:13:56,867 --> 01:13:58,768
- Just think,
no school ever again.

1843
01:13:58,835 --> 01:13:59,903
- Now listen.

1844
01:14:01,272 --> 01:14:02,472
You’ve been scared, Tommy.

1845
01:14:02,538 --> 01:14:04,407
I know that,
and it’s my fault.

1846
01:14:04,475 --> 01:14:05,942
- Because I tried to kill you.

1847
01:14:06,008 --> 01:14:09,212
- That’s, that’s because
I was scared, too,

1848
01:14:09,278 --> 01:14:11,882
so scared that I,
I almost lost my head.

1849
01:14:11,948 --> 01:14:13,050
- Here, look, I wet them.
Now do you believe me?

1850
01:14:13,117 --> 01:14:15,252
- A man can be scared, too,
Tommy,

1851
01:14:15,318 --> 01:14:17,353
even of a kid like you,

1852
01:14:17,420 --> 01:14:20,657
especially if that kid gets
the wrong idea in his head;

1853
01:14:20,723 --> 01:14:23,393
and you’ve got
the wrong idea, son.

1854
01:14:23,460 --> 01:14:24,828
- Yeah.
- You lied.

1855
01:14:24,895 --> 01:14:28,598
- You utterly lied.
- You said he was hunting.

1856
01:14:28,665 --> 01:14:31,000
- Yes, yes, I lied.

1857
01:14:31,067 --> 01:14:32,635
- You made me lie.

1858
01:14:32,702 --> 01:14:35,238
- Because I knew
you loved Jonathan, Tommy,

1859
01:14:35,305 --> 01:14:37,807
almost as much as
your own father.

1860
01:14:37,875 --> 01:14:41,812
So, I, well, I didn’t know
how to tell you just like that.

1861
01:14:41,878 --> 01:14:43,747
- Am I soothing you, Tommy?

1862
01:14:43,813 --> 01:14:45,182
- Jonathan is dead, son.

1863
01:14:45,248 --> 01:14:46,717
- And I helped.

1864
01:14:46,783 --> 01:14:48,752
- He was working up
in the canyon, and he fell.

1865
01:14:48,818 --> 01:14:50,720
- After I pushed him.
- It was an accident.

1866
01:14:50,787 --> 01:14:52,422
- Shep told me to kill him.

1867
01:14:52,488 --> 01:14:54,023
- Two days ago,
I found his body.

1868
01:14:54,090 --> 01:14:55,492
- And I laughed.

1869
01:14:55,558 --> 01:14:57,360
- Well, I buried it
where you saw.

1870
01:14:57,427 --> 01:14:58,762
- Oh, I buried him, yeah.

1871
01:14:58,828 --> 01:15:00,097
- I was coming in tomorrow
to tell your ma.

1872
01:15:00,163 --> 01:15:01,365
- If you want anymore facts,

1873
01:15:01,432 --> 01:15:02,598
you’ll have to speak
to my lawyer.

1874
01:15:03,900 --> 01:15:05,268
- Shep.

1875
01:15:05,335 --> 01:15:06,837
- Shep doesn’t like you, Tommy.
She told me that.

1876
01:15:06,903 --> 01:15:09,773
- Where’s Shep?
- Why, Shep’s all right.

1877
01:15:09,840 --> 01:15:11,275
I saw her outside
a while ago.

1878
01:15:11,342 --> 01:15:13,743
- She’s cool.
- Shep was like you, son.

1879
01:15:13,810 --> 01:15:16,213
- Bad.
- She got the wrong idea, too.

1880
01:15:16,280 --> 01:15:17,613
- Oh, yeah.

1881
01:15:17,680 --> 01:15:18,882
- So, let’s,
let’s both of us forget

1882
01:15:18,948 --> 01:15:22,452
about what happened tonight,
shall we?

1883
01:15:22,518 --> 01:15:23,453
- Bury it deep--
- Right.

1884
01:15:23,520 --> 01:15:25,322
- --real deep, Tommy.

1885
01:15:25,388 --> 01:15:28,858
- I guess so.
- Sure we will.

1886
01:15:28,925 --> 01:15:30,427
Now, you get some sleep,

1887
01:15:30,493 --> 01:15:32,128
and you’ll be fine
in the morning.

1888
01:15:32,195 --> 01:15:35,332
( Sinister laughing )

1889
01:15:41,170 --> 01:15:44,240
Just sleep, son,
and forget the whole thing.

1890
01:15:44,307 --> 01:15:47,410
- Mm, honk shoe
honk shoe, honk shoe, hm.

1891
01:15:49,813 --> 01:15:50,847
PETE:
Ho!

1892
01:15:50,913 --> 01:15:54,083
( Bots yelling like tarzan )

1893
01:15:54,150 --> 01:15:57,053
Hello!

1894
01:15:57,120 --> 01:16:00,257
- Hey, there’s a really loud
French guy out there.

1895
01:16:00,323 --> 01:16:01,758
- Hello there!

1896
01:16:01,825 --> 01:16:04,527
- Oh, no, Pile On Pete,
Pile On Jack, Pile On Roadie.

1897
01:16:05,928 --> 01:16:07,563
- Tommy, wake up.
- The Pile Ons are here.

1898
01:16:07,630 --> 01:16:09,098
- We have company, son.

1899
01:16:09,165 --> 01:16:10,667
And don’t forget what
we talked about last night.

1900
01:16:10,733 --> 01:16:12,368
About the accident
to Jonathan.

1901
01:16:12,435 --> 01:16:14,905
Better let me do
all the explaining, all right?

1902
01:16:14,972 --> 01:16:17,473
PETE:
Hey, Jonathan!

1903
01:16:17,540 --> 01:16:20,243
- What’s happening?

1904
01:16:20,310 --> 01:16:24,380
- Hi, Mr. Taylor.
Mel Steiner and Carl Crane.

1905
01:16:24,447 --> 01:16:25,515
- They’re with the DA.

1906
01:16:25,582 --> 01:16:26,983
- This runaway
come into their camp

1907
01:16:27,050 --> 01:16:28,552
over in Oak Flats last night,

1908
01:16:28,618 --> 01:16:30,753
and I thought I remembered
he was yours.

1909
01:16:30,820 --> 01:16:32,722
They was breaking camp
for winter anyway;

1910
01:16:32,788 --> 01:16:34,323
so, on the way into town,

1911
01:16:34,390 --> 01:16:36,627
figured we’d cut across
the ridge and fetch him back.

1912
01:16:36,693 --> 01:16:38,062
- Maybe pile on you.

1913
01:16:38,128 --> 01:16:39,730
- Why, hello, there,
Tommy boy.

1914
01:16:39,797 --> 01:16:40,963
- He’s a murderer!
- Hi, Mr. Pete.

1915
01:16:41,030 --> 01:16:42,798
Gee, I’m sure glad to see you.

1916
01:16:42,865 --> 01:16:44,533
- We both are, Pete,
mighty glad,

1917
01:16:44,600 --> 01:16:46,168
and thanks for bringing
the horse back.

1918
01:16:46,235 --> 01:16:49,105
- Sure. Where’s Jonathan?

1919
01:16:49,172 --> 01:16:50,873
- It’s a good thing
you men came along.

1920
01:16:50,940 --> 01:16:52,408
Come inside.

1921
01:16:52,475 --> 01:16:53,543
I’m afraid I’ve got
some bad news for you.

1922
01:16:53,610 --> 01:16:55,112
- Remember last week
when he was alive?

1923
01:16:55,178 --> 01:16:56,647
Well, that didn’t last.

1924
01:16:56,713 --> 01:16:59,750
PETE: And in thy
heavenly kingdom, Lord,

1925
01:16:59,817 --> 01:17:01,785
let him find peace.

1926
01:17:01,852 --> 01:17:03,253
- And let him pile on.

1927
01:17:03,320 --> 01:17:05,155
PETE: That peace
which passeth understanding.

1928
01:17:05,222 --> 01:17:06,690
- At least I don’t get it.

1929
01:17:06,757 --> 01:17:11,027
PETE: Let the gold he sought
on earth be his in heaven,

1930
01:17:11,093 --> 01:17:14,530
the pure and shining gold
of thy holy grace.

1931
01:17:14,597 --> 01:17:16,198
- Let’s skip a bit, brother.

1932
01:17:16,265 --> 01:17:18,568
- We ask it, Lord--
- Very slowly.

1933
01:17:18,635 --> 01:17:22,005
- --in His name, amen.

1934
01:17:22,072 --> 01:17:24,540
- Now the jets will fly
in the missing man formation.

1935
01:17:24,607 --> 01:17:26,643
( Bot making jet sounds )

1936
01:17:29,145 --> 01:17:31,415
- Well, custom says
we throw a handful of dirt,

1937
01:17:31,482 --> 01:17:33,083
but I’ve got a wicked idea.

1938
01:17:33,150 --> 01:17:34,650
- Oh, no, no.

1939
01:17:34,718 --> 01:17:35,718
- Come along, Tommy.

1940
01:17:35,785 --> 01:17:37,353
- Don’t pile on me.

1941
01:17:40,723 --> 01:17:42,725
- Let’s turn to a life of crime,
Tommy.

1942
01:17:44,762 --> 01:17:45,962
- Come on, Shep.

1943
01:17:46,028 --> 01:17:47,763
- Thanks, Pete,
and I’ll count on you

1944
01:17:47,830 --> 01:17:49,632
to explain everything in town.

1945
01:17:49,700 --> 01:17:51,200
- That I will.

1946
01:17:51,267 --> 01:17:53,203
- As long as you can take Tommy
and the dog back for me,

1947
01:17:53,270 --> 01:17:55,372
I’ll probably stay on up here
’til spring.

1948
01:17:55,438 --> 01:17:56,407
- Right.

1949
01:17:56,473 --> 01:17:57,740
TAYLOR:
For everyone’s sake,

1950
01:17:57,807 --> 01:17:58,842
I’d like to prove
Jonathan was right.

1951
01:17:58,908 --> 01:18:00,410
- Yeah, worked it.

1952
01:18:00,477 --> 01:18:01,545
TAYLOR: You see, he was sure
there was real pay dirt here.

1953
01:18:01,612 --> 01:18:02,812
- Rave on.

1954
01:18:02,878 --> 01:18:04,247
TAYLOR: So far,
we’ve never found it.

1955
01:18:04,313 --> 01:18:06,115
And, Pete, do all you can
to help Tommy, will you?

1956
01:18:06,182 --> 01:18:07,650
- Adults suck.

1957
01:18:07,717 --> 01:18:09,753
TAYLOR: You know, kids sometimes
imagine all sorts of things,

1958
01:18:09,820 --> 01:18:11,422
and Jonathan’s accident
hit the boy pretty hard.

1959
01:18:11,488 --> 01:18:12,588
- So did I.

1960
01:18:12,655 --> 01:18:13,790
TAYLOR:
I’m sure you understand.

1961
01:18:13,857 --> 01:18:15,958
PETE:
I do indeed, Mr. Taylor.

1962
01:18:16,025 --> 01:18:17,527
- Pete, he’s lying!
Don’t believe him!

1963
01:18:17,593 --> 01:18:19,930
Please don’t believe him!
It wasn’t an accident.

1964
01:18:19,997 --> 01:18:21,363
Jonathan didn’t fall.
He killed him.

1965
01:18:21,432 --> 01:18:23,367
I know he did.
Pete, he’s lying!

1966
01:18:23,433 --> 01:18:25,068
He’s lying about everything!
- Tommy.

1967
01:18:25,135 --> 01:18:26,570
- Kids say
the darnedest things, eh?

1968
01:18:26,637 --> 01:18:27,870
- The boy’s hysterical.

1969
01:18:27,937 --> 01:18:29,538
- He hated Jonathan,
and he killed him;

1970
01:18:29,605 --> 01:18:30,640
and last night,
when I found out,

1971
01:18:30,707 --> 01:18:31,942
he tried to kill me, too.

1972
01:18:32,008 --> 01:18:33,143
He’s lying, Pete.

1973
01:18:33,210 --> 01:18:34,410
- Tommy, listen to me.

1974
01:18:34,477 --> 01:18:36,012
You say
I wanted to kill you?

1975
01:18:36,078 --> 01:18:37,880
Where did you sleep
last night?

1976
01:18:37,947 --> 01:18:39,348
Who picked you up,
when your horse threw you,

1977
01:18:39,415 --> 01:18:41,217
and carried you to the cabin?

1978
01:18:41,283 --> 01:18:43,720
Tommy, you slept six feet from
me the whole night through,

1979
01:18:43,787 --> 01:18:44,822
safe and well.

1980
01:18:44,888 --> 01:18:46,122
- I didn’t even kill ya.

1981
01:18:46,188 --> 01:18:47,223
- Does that sound like
I wanted to hurt you?

1982
01:18:47,290 --> 01:18:48,658
Answer me.

1983
01:18:48,725 --> 01:18:50,293
- Pete, don’t believe him!
He did do it!

1984
01:18:50,360 --> 01:18:51,528
Shep knows, too.

1985
01:18:51,595 --> 01:18:52,762
That’s why he tried
to poison her!

1986
01:18:52,828 --> 01:18:55,132
- You want I should
pile on him here?

1987
01:18:56,600 --> 01:18:59,335
- You don’t believe me.
- Nope.

1988
01:18:59,402 --> 01:19:02,638
- He said the claim
didn’t pay out, but it did.

1989
01:19:02,705 --> 01:19:06,208
There’s bags full of gold
in that cabin, a dozen of them.

1990
01:19:06,275 --> 01:19:08,245
- If that’s true,
I guess you can show us, son.

1991
01:19:08,312 --> 01:19:09,512
- I will, come on.

1992
01:19:09,578 --> 01:19:10,947
- Mm, this is turning into
a parlor mystery.

1993
01:19:11,013 --> 01:19:12,582
I bet there’s gonna be
three different endings.

1994
01:19:14,985 --> 01:19:17,253
- It’s just as bright indoors
as out.

1995
01:19:21,692 --> 01:19:23,160
- Faced.

1996
01:19:23,227 --> 01:19:27,130
- There was more,
a lot more.

1997
01:19:27,197 --> 01:19:28,298
He’s done something
with the rest.

1998
01:19:28,365 --> 01:19:30,033
- He’s been eating coal a lot,
sorry.

1999
01:19:32,502 --> 01:19:34,670
- You’d better take these
back to Martha.

2000
01:19:34,737 --> 01:19:36,138
I was holding this,

2001
01:19:36,205 --> 01:19:38,742
hoping that I’d have
a bigger haul by spring.

2002
01:19:38,808 --> 01:19:40,810
- There is more now,
a lot more.

2003
01:19:40,877 --> 01:19:42,278
- Shut your hole, Tommy.

2004
01:19:42,345 --> 01:19:44,213
- Mr. Crane,
can’t you see he’s lying?

2005
01:19:44,280 --> 01:19:46,282
Mr. Steiner,
don’t believe him.

2006
01:19:46,348 --> 01:19:48,117
Please don’t believe him.

2007
01:19:48,185 --> 01:19:50,520
- I guess
we’d better be going.

2008
01:19:50,587 --> 01:19:52,822
- Please, Mr. Pete.
- Tommy, don’t.

2009
01:19:52,888 --> 01:19:54,958
You’re embarrassing me.
- Come along, son.

2010
01:19:57,160 --> 01:19:58,262
- You’ll be hearing
from my lawyer, kid.

2011
01:19:58,328 --> 01:19:59,628
We’re talking money.

2012
01:19:59,695 --> 01:20:02,632
( Music playing )

2013
01:20:05,968 --> 01:20:07,203
TOMMY:
Come on, Shep.

2014
01:20:09,072 --> 01:20:12,508
- I know where you live, Taylor.
Time to die.

2015
01:20:12,575 --> 01:20:15,012
- Lady, come on, come on.

2016
01:20:15,078 --> 01:20:17,213
- I’m barking like a canary,
Taylor!

2017
01:20:19,448 --> 01:20:22,218
- Never piled on a dog before.

2018
01:20:22,285 --> 01:20:23,787
- There’s not a pound
that’ll hold me, Taylor!

2019
01:20:23,853 --> 01:20:25,188
PETE: Lady, come on.
- I’ll be back.

2020
01:20:25,255 --> 01:20:28,157
( Theme music playing )

2021
01:20:30,660 --> 01:20:31,828
( Shep growling )

2022
01:20:31,895 --> 01:20:33,797
- Wild dogs can’t be broken.

2023
01:20:33,863 --> 01:20:35,598
TOMMY: Shep, come back!
Shep!

2024
01:20:35,665 --> 01:20:38,033
- You’ll never pile on me,
old man!

2025
01:20:41,572 --> 01:20:43,473
- The celebration
starts early.

2026
01:20:43,540 --> 01:20:46,543
( Shep barking )

2027
01:20:47,943 --> 01:20:50,680
- Oh, Mr. Shep, you have
a nasty habit of surviving.

2028
01:20:54,317 --> 01:20:58,855
- All right, throw the Snausages
out nice and easy.

2029
01:20:58,922 --> 01:21:01,023
( Gunshot )

2030
01:21:01,090 --> 01:21:03,158
Prepare to meet Kali.

2031
01:21:07,997 --> 01:21:09,732
- Mm, smart dog.

2032
01:21:09,798 --> 01:21:12,668
Took Taylor out of his season,
so he’s vulnerable.

2033
01:21:12,735 --> 01:21:13,870
- Hey, wait a second.

2034
01:21:13,937 --> 01:21:16,338
I think Barbasol had
an industrial spill.

2035
01:21:16,405 --> 01:21:17,440
- Oh.
( SHEP BARKING )

2036
01:21:17,507 --> 01:21:18,742
- That’s weird.

2037
01:21:20,377 --> 01:21:21,443
- Come on, dummy.

2038
01:21:27,283 --> 01:21:28,318
( GUNSHOT )
Woof!

2039
01:21:28,385 --> 01:21:30,153
Can taste the ozone
on that one.

2040
01:21:33,423 --> 01:21:35,792
Okay, the DA is supposed to
meet me here.

2041
01:21:37,660 --> 01:21:40,297
Ooh, ooh, that’s cold.
Oh, boy.

2042
01:21:40,363 --> 01:21:44,167
Oh, my dogs are killing me.
Ooh, ah, ooh!

2043
01:21:44,233 --> 01:21:46,335
This is no place
for a convertible!

2044
01:21:48,405 --> 01:21:51,642
- She’s taking him up K2,
see, because she’s a canine.

2045
01:21:54,810 --> 01:21:56,813
Watch out for squibs, Lassie!

2046
01:22:00,617 --> 01:22:01,985
Hey, Lassie got down
on all fours.

2047
01:22:02,052 --> 01:22:03,320
Let’s see this guy do that.

2048
01:22:06,890 --> 01:22:08,692
- Oh, look at all
the footprints.

2049
01:22:08,758 --> 01:22:10,660
They’re at a ski resort.

2050
01:22:10,727 --> 01:22:11,662
- Hm.

2051
01:22:13,597 --> 01:22:15,365
- Oh.
( GUNSHOT )

2052
01:22:15,432 --> 01:22:17,467
- Hey, what are you,
Claudine Longet?

2053
01:22:18,968 --> 01:22:20,003
- I know what you’re thinking.

2054
01:22:20,070 --> 01:22:22,438
Did I fire six shots
or only five?

2055
01:22:22,505 --> 01:22:23,507
Well, this film
is so poorly made,

2056
01:22:23,573 --> 01:22:25,008
it doesn’t matter.

2057
01:22:29,312 --> 01:22:31,347
( Shep barking )

2058
01:22:32,582 --> 01:22:33,950
( Bot making spitting sounds )

2059
01:22:34,017 --> 01:22:36,452
- I’m going to teach you
the meaning of arf.

2060
01:22:38,253 --> 01:22:40,890
- The Fido Sanction--
- Where beagles dare.

2061
01:22:40,957 --> 01:22:42,225
( Laughing )

2062
01:22:44,960 --> 01:22:46,595
- Three years later.

2063
01:22:46,663 --> 01:22:48,365
( Gunshots )

2064
01:22:49,898 --> 01:22:52,568
- Oh, now she’s gonna have
to be saved by a St. Bernard.

2065
01:22:52,635 --> 01:22:53,570
- Hm...

2066
01:22:55,072 --> 01:22:56,907
The most tedious game.

2067
01:22:56,973 --> 01:23:00,610
( Suspenseful music )

2068
01:23:00,677 --> 01:23:02,545
- Ugh.

2069
01:23:02,612 --> 01:23:04,447
- Hey, he’s turning into
Michael McDonald.

2070
01:23:04,513 --> 01:23:05,448
- That’s right.

2071
01:23:07,450 --> 01:23:09,718
- Stashed a bag of Cycle Two
up here somewhere.

2072
01:23:11,487 --> 01:23:12,888
Wait a minute.
Now, what was my plan?

2073
01:23:12,955 --> 01:23:15,892
I started chasing a rabbit,
and I forgot what I was doing.

2074
01:23:19,728 --> 01:23:23,633
- ♪ She came from somewhere
out of the long ago ♪

2075
01:23:25,668 --> 01:23:26,937
You know, it’s warm
in the foothills.

2076
01:23:27,003 --> 01:23:28,070
If you’re going out to
chase a dog today,

2077
01:23:28,137 --> 01:23:30,573
you might want to
take a jacket.

2078
01:23:30,640 --> 01:23:34,477
- Looks like a good place
to end this little charade.

2079
01:23:34,543 --> 01:23:37,213
- Uh, this is no good.
We’re on top of the monument.

2080
01:23:38,715 --> 01:23:40,550
- You know, they did
a lot of takes there.

2081
01:23:42,285 --> 01:23:43,352
- This is where
Holmes met his end,

2082
01:23:43,420 --> 01:23:44,587
at Reichenbach Falls.

2083
01:23:44,653 --> 01:23:45,955
- Oh, yeah,
Reichenbach, Texas.

2084
01:23:46,022 --> 01:23:47,257
- No, no,
that’s Luckenbach, Texas.

2085
01:23:47,323 --> 01:23:48,257
- So, it’s Luckenbach Falls?

2086
01:23:48,323 --> 01:23:50,527
- Never mind.
- It’s--

2087
01:23:50,593 --> 01:23:52,195
- Well, his hair turned gray
so gradually,

2088
01:23:52,262 --> 01:23:53,763
I didn’t notice it.

2089
01:23:55,398 --> 01:23:56,967
- This is like the end
of "The Shootist."

2090
01:23:57,033 --> 01:23:59,335
- All right, Lassie,
make your move.

2091
01:24:01,670 --> 01:24:04,607
( Bot whistling
and suspenseful music )

2092
01:24:10,080 --> 01:24:12,115
- This is gonna work, steady.

2093
01:24:12,182 --> 01:24:14,150
- He’s turned into Jonathan.

2094
01:24:15,918 --> 01:24:18,655
- Uh, uh, Russ, Russ,
this guy’s serious, Russ.

2095
01:24:18,722 --> 01:24:19,655
Where are you?

2096
01:24:21,958 --> 01:24:23,292
- Check out
your left shoe, mister.

2097
01:24:23,358 --> 01:24:25,027
You stepped in my pee!

2098
01:24:25,095 --> 01:24:26,128
( Sinister laughing )

2099
01:24:28,330 --> 01:24:30,367
- Alex Trebek, no!

2100
01:24:34,203 --> 01:24:35,838
- Gun’s turned into
a bomb pop!

2101
01:24:39,308 --> 01:24:41,945
- Oh, some kind of problem?

2102
01:24:42,012 --> 01:24:43,547
- Wait a minute.

2103
01:24:43,613 --> 01:24:45,748
I’ve hit absolute zero,
and I’m still walking around.

2104
01:24:45,815 --> 01:24:47,617
How’d I do that?

2105
01:24:47,683 --> 01:24:49,385
- Snausage, honey?

2106
01:24:49,452 --> 01:24:51,788
Now smile for me,
golden boy.

2107
01:24:57,427 --> 01:25:01,230
- Slowly I turned,
step by step.

2108
01:25:02,398 --> 01:25:03,398
- This was really cheap.

2109
01:25:03,467 --> 01:25:04,867
They just reversed the film.

2110
01:25:04,933 --> 01:25:07,303
- Now what, is he going to run
all the way back down now?

2111
01:25:07,370 --> 01:25:08,672
- Hey, wait a minute.

2112
01:25:08,738 --> 01:25:09,738
Aren’t they supposed to turn
into a big snowball

2113
01:25:09,805 --> 01:25:11,107
when they fall like that?

2114
01:25:11,173 --> 01:25:12,108
- Yeah.

2115
01:25:14,910 --> 01:25:17,347
- Oh, for the want of a Frisbee.

2116
01:25:17,413 --> 01:25:19,182
Friends?
Nice pooch.

2117
01:25:19,248 --> 01:25:20,317
- Never let a dog
sense your fear.

2118
01:25:20,383 --> 01:25:22,852
Offer him your hand.

2119
01:25:22,918 --> 01:25:25,120
- Oh, he’s offering
some prime butt to bite.

2120
01:25:25,188 --> 01:25:26,422
- Hoo-hoo.

2121
01:25:26,488 --> 01:25:30,092
( Suspenseful music )

2122
01:25:32,428 --> 01:25:34,763
- Oh, man, this is
one tough ski kid.

2123
01:25:34,830 --> 01:25:35,965
- Mm-hm.

2124
01:25:36,032 --> 01:25:37,267
- He’s in Camelot.

2125
01:25:37,333 --> 01:25:39,602
- Merlin, Merlin,
turn me into a bird

2126
01:25:39,668 --> 01:25:42,438
so I may fly from here.

2127
01:25:42,505 --> 01:25:44,873
- Wait a minute.
Lassie started a forest fire?

2128
01:25:44,940 --> 01:25:47,043
The plan’s getting
more elaborate all the time.

2129
01:25:47,110 --> 01:25:50,280
- Wow, and he’s turned
all the way into Lon Chaney, Jr.

2130
01:25:50,347 --> 01:25:53,950
( Suspenseful music )

2131
01:25:54,017 --> 01:25:56,887
- Oh, perfect.
This fits right into my day!

2132
01:25:58,287 --> 01:26:00,623
- Wait a minute. All I have
to do is hang glide down

2133
01:26:00,690 --> 01:26:02,458
and have a cool
Canadian club.

2134
01:26:02,525 --> 01:26:03,458
- Hm.

2135
01:26:05,228 --> 01:26:07,530
- Shep, is this supposed to be
poetic justice?

2136
01:26:07,597 --> 01:26:09,598
I don’t do poetic justice.

2137
01:26:09,665 --> 01:26:13,637
- No, Shep. I swear
it was an accident.

2138
01:26:13,703 --> 01:26:15,572
No, I didn’t do it.

2139
01:26:15,638 --> 01:26:17,273
- Guys, this couldn’t be lamer.

2140
01:26:17,340 --> 01:26:18,608
- No.
( SHEP GROWLING )

2141
01:26:18,675 --> 01:26:20,042
Don’t do it.
Don’t do it, please.

2142
01:26:20,108 --> 01:26:22,712
- I mean, the dog should make
his brain explode, scanner dog.

2143
01:26:22,778 --> 01:26:23,880
That I would buy.

2144
01:26:23,947 --> 01:26:25,180
- Ooh, that’d be cool.

2145
01:26:25,248 --> 01:26:28,585
- Please, Shep,
please, please!

2146
01:26:28,652 --> 01:26:30,253
Shep, no, no!

2147
01:26:30,320 --> 01:26:33,723
- Grizzly Adams.
- I didn’t mean it, no, no!

2148
01:26:33,790 --> 01:26:36,158
- You don’t know me, but-- ah!

2149
01:26:36,225 --> 01:26:39,028
Ow, ooh, ooh,
ooh, ow, ooh, ow.

2150
01:26:39,095 --> 01:26:41,965
- Oh, gun’s loose now--
thanks!

2151
01:26:43,065 --> 01:26:45,368
- License revoked.

2152
01:26:46,768 --> 01:26:48,470
- We should’ve tried
the bunny hill first.

2153
01:26:53,275 --> 01:26:55,478
- Heh, heh, heh, gotta laugh.
Poor bastard.

2154
01:26:55,545 --> 01:26:57,480
Well, gotta do my business.

2155
01:26:58,982 --> 01:27:02,852
- Mm, thought I’d feel good,
but I feel kind of empty.

2156
01:27:03,953 --> 01:27:05,322
- Folks, folks,
I had to do it.

2157
01:27:05,388 --> 01:27:06,990
He killed my best friend,
and he poisoned me.

2158
01:27:07,057 --> 01:27:09,425
I can’t testify in court.
I’m a dog!

2159
01:27:10,827 --> 01:27:13,195
Actually, I accomplished
quite a bit today.

2160
01:27:14,563 --> 01:27:17,433
- Shep! Here, Shep!

2161
01:27:17,500 --> 01:27:19,935
- Oh, look, they’re inside
one of those shaky snow worlds.

2162
01:27:20,002 --> 01:27:21,537
- Mm.

2163
01:27:21,603 --> 01:27:24,373
- She’s lost, Pete.
We’ll never find her.

2164
01:27:24,440 --> 01:27:26,108
- We’ll keep trying, son.

2165
01:27:26,175 --> 01:27:29,045
- So, what exactly does Tommy
get out of all this?

2166
01:27:31,380 --> 01:27:32,315
Hm?

2167
01:27:33,382 --> 01:27:34,850
- My mouth’s bleeding, Bert.

2168
01:27:36,352 --> 01:27:38,287
- Now that I think of it,
this doesn’t look so good.

2169
01:27:38,353 --> 01:27:41,123
Let’s see, what can I say?

2170
01:27:41,190 --> 01:27:44,193
( Wind howling )

2171
01:27:45,562 --> 01:27:49,365
Oh, hi, Pile On Pete.
Got some Snausages?

2172
01:27:49,432 --> 01:27:52,935
- And so the little fellow
found the murderous collie.

2173
01:27:53,002 --> 01:27:55,372
- Shep, what’s wrong?

2174
01:27:55,438 --> 01:27:58,007
- You’ve killed again,
haven’t you?

2175
01:27:58,073 --> 01:28:01,843
- It’s all right, Shep.
Sure it is.

2176
01:28:01,910 --> 01:28:04,113
- I didn’t do it, honest.

2177
01:28:04,180 --> 01:28:05,982
My ears don’t wiggle
when I’m lying.

2178
01:28:09,052 --> 01:28:11,020
- "The Painted Hills,
Blood Quest 2:

2179
01:28:11,087 --> 01:28:15,190
Some Things They Don’t Teach
in Obedience School."

2180
01:28:15,257 --> 01:28:18,627
- I’m having a personal
pants pile on.

2181
01:28:20,295 --> 01:28:22,665
- Look out, Tommy! He’s learned
the taste of human blood.

2182
01:28:24,767 --> 01:28:26,268
- All that was filmed
in Hollywood?

2183
01:28:26,335 --> 01:28:28,503
And how did they do that?
Hollywood?

2184
01:28:28,570 --> 01:28:30,707
- Didn’t look like
Hollywood to me.

2185
01:28:30,773 --> 01:28:32,642
- Oh, Pilot Pete!
Pilot with a "t".

2186
01:28:32,708 --> 01:28:35,778
- See, we thought
it was Pile On.

2187
01:28:35,845 --> 01:28:37,780
- Are our faces red?
( LAUGHING )

2188
01:28:37,847 --> 01:28:39,315
- I’m the ass.

2189
01:28:39,382 --> 01:28:41,617
- Wait, one more.

2190
01:28:41,683 --> 01:28:42,952
Ah, thank you.

2191
01:28:51,993 --> 01:28:54,130
- Well, that movie had
an interesting message.

2192
01:28:54,197 --> 01:28:56,498
- Yeah, that any flea bag’s
white hot vendetta

2193
01:28:56,565 --> 01:28:59,202
should go unchecked
until the last man lay bleeding

2194
01:28:59,268 --> 01:29:01,337
at the bottom of a ravine
with a crushed skull.

2195
01:29:01,403 --> 01:29:02,672
- Yeah, that’s what I got
out of it, too, Joel.

2196
01:29:02,738 --> 01:29:04,007
- Oh, come on, you guys.

2197
01:29:04,073 --> 01:29:06,242
Lassie was just doing
what she thought was right.

2198
01:29:06,308 --> 01:29:08,343
- Yeah, well, who died
and made that scabby mutt

2199
01:29:08,410 --> 01:29:10,480
judge, jury,
and executioner, hm?

2200
01:29:10,547 --> 01:29:12,515
- Well, somebody had to
bring that guy to justice.

2201
01:29:12,582 --> 01:29:14,417
- Oh, so is that the message,
Joel?

2202
01:29:14,483 --> 01:29:15,618
Take the law
into your own hands,

2203
01:29:15,685 --> 01:29:17,887
even if you drink from
a bowl on the floor?

2204
01:29:17,953 --> 01:29:19,688
- No, well-- maybe.

2205
01:29:19,755 --> 01:29:21,057
- Yeah, well, I say

2206
01:29:21,123 --> 01:29:22,958
if that DA in that jerk wadded
two-horse mining town

2207
01:29:23,025 --> 01:29:24,260
had any guts,

2208
01:29:24,327 --> 01:29:26,195
he’d bring that shaggy butcher
up on murder one

2209
01:29:26,262 --> 01:29:28,097
and make sure she never
nuzzled another crotch.

2210
01:29:28,163 --> 01:29:29,432
- Yeah.
- You know what I mean?

2211
01:29:29,498 --> 01:29:31,667
- Yeah, but she saw
Taylor murder Jonathan.

2212
01:29:31,733 --> 01:29:33,668
She was hurt.
She had to lash out.

2213
01:29:33,737 --> 01:29:35,438
- Oh, great.
So, by your code,

2214
01:29:35,505 --> 01:29:38,742
I accidentally bump my
Rottweiler with a screen door,

2215
01:29:38,808 --> 01:29:42,043
I wake up the next morning with
a severed cat head in my bed.

2216
01:29:42,112 --> 01:29:43,045
Oh, good one, Joel.

2217
01:29:43,112 --> 01:29:44,147
- Face it, Joel.

2218
01:29:44,213 --> 01:29:45,548
Your precious Lassie
should be doing

2219
01:29:45,615 --> 01:29:48,250
consecutive life sentences
at the Crowbar Motel.

2220
01:29:48,317 --> 01:29:50,420
- Yep, we’ve got a body,
we’ve got a motive.

2221
01:29:50,487 --> 01:29:51,820
The paw prints
and blood stains

2222
01:29:51,887 --> 01:29:53,622
put her at the scene
of the crime.

2223
01:29:53,688 --> 01:29:55,758
- We’ve got witnesses
that saw her threaten him.

2224
01:29:55,825 --> 01:29:58,327
We’ve got a handful of people
who saw her slip her tether

2225
01:29:58,393 --> 01:29:59,795
and go after him.

2226
01:29:59,862 --> 01:30:02,632
That cur is gonna fry, Joel,
fry.

2227
01:30:02,698 --> 01:30:03,867
- What about a murder weapon?

2228
01:30:03,933 --> 01:30:06,068
- Her own devious mind.

2229
01:30:06,135 --> 01:30:08,003
- No deals on this one,
Robinson.

2230
01:30:08,070 --> 01:30:10,138
Your client’s gonna fall.
She’s gonna fall hard.

2231
01:30:10,205 --> 01:30:11,507
- Oh, yeah?
- Yeah.

2232
01:30:11,573 --> 01:30:13,108
- Well, there ain’t gonna be
any deal this time.

2233
01:30:13,175 --> 01:30:14,777
You’re going to the mat
on this one!

2234
01:30:14,843 --> 01:30:15,978
Listen, let me tell you,
you’ve got nothing!

2235
01:30:16,045 --> 01:30:17,247
- Jail, jail, jail--

2236
01:30:17,313 --> 01:30:18,380
- You’ve got nothing,
and you’re scared!

2237
01:30:18,447 --> 01:30:19,815
Why, you’re just
blowing smoke up--

2238
01:30:19,882 --> 01:30:21,917
listen, I’m not gonna get sucked
into this with you guys.

2239
01:30:21,983 --> 01:30:25,420
Listen, Lassie is an animal and,
therefore, not subject to

2240
01:30:25,488 --> 01:30:27,990
the same moral or ethical code
that human beings are;

2241
01:30:28,057 --> 01:30:30,760
otherwise, they’d all be
arrested for public nudity.

2242
01:30:30,827 --> 01:30:32,662
- Oh, got a point.

2243
01:30:32,728 --> 01:30:34,530
- Yeah, makes sense.

2244
01:30:34,597 --> 01:30:37,633
- What do you think, sirs?
I’m going to bed.

2245
01:30:37,700 --> 01:30:39,368
- Ugh, ugh!

2246
01:30:39,435 --> 01:30:40,368
Frank?

2247
01:30:41,437 --> 01:30:45,307
Frank, Frank, Frank, no!

2248
01:30:45,373 --> 01:30:46,308
Hi, Joel.

2249
01:30:46,375 --> 01:30:49,178
Uh, Frank’s dead again.

2250
01:30:49,245 --> 01:30:51,080
Well, I’ve gotta go out
and buy him a new heart now,

2251
01:30:51,147 --> 01:30:53,950
like there’s not enough
I’ve gotta do around here.

2252
01:30:54,017 --> 01:30:56,885
- Ohh.
- I’m coming-- geez!

2253
01:30:59,788 --> 01:31:02,725
( Theme music playing )

